/* Chapter 18 — canonical topics, questions, flashcards and exam prompts. */
IFL_DATA.registerChapter({"number":18,"title":"The Way Forward","part":"part-iii","partRoman":"III","pages":[457,480],"minutes":60,"estimates":{"f1":60,"f2":65},"difficulty":"Intermediate","difficultyAlt":"advanced","learningObjectives":[{"id":"lo-ch18-1","text":"Explain the author's diagnosis of global financial problems and the two-part agenda of the chapter.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch18-2","text":"State the international policy measures proposed (reserve currencies, money creation, replacing loan-based aid, ethical appeal).","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch18-3","text":"Explain the role expected of Muslim states and a phased transformation plan.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch18-4","text":"Identify the driving forces and potential of Islamic finance (fund management, Sukuk, SMEs and microfinance).","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch18-5","text":"Analyse issues: Shari’ah interpretation, integrity and credibility, structure, regulation and tax, Shari’ah compliance frameworks (Box 18.1).","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch18-6","text":"Evaluate challenges: awareness, compliance and integrity, competitiveness, benchmarks, product development, liquidity and monetary management, human resources.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch18-7","text":"Explain the author's macro-level policy agenda for disciplining money/credit creation and financial systems","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch18-8","text":"Describe the specific reforms the author recommends for Muslim states regarding Islamic finance adoption","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch18-9","text":"Identify the factors that have driven Islamic finance's rapid growth and its potential in fund management, Sukuk and specific sectors","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch18-10","text":"Analyse the key structural, regulatory and credibility issues facing Islamic finance's continued development","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch18-11","text":"Evaluate the options for a Shari'ah compliance framework and the major challenges facing the industry's future","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch18-12","text":"Synthesize the book's overall conclusions and recommendations for Islamic banking and finance's sustainable growth","origin":["f2"]}],"why":"Chapter 18 draws the book together into policy recommendations. It is the natural source for essays on ‘the future of Islamic banking’, ‘issues and challenges’ or ‘Shari’ah governance frameworks’, including SBP's framework in Box 18.1.","whyExpanded":"As the book's concluding chapter, this material ties together the theoretical foundations, product mechanics and critical appraisals of the previous seventeen chapters into a forward-looking agenda. It equips students to think like a policymaker or practitioner assessing what Islamic finance still needs - structurally, regulatorily and in terms of human capital - to fulfil its stated socio-economic promise, rather than treating the industry's current state as its final form.","overview":"The author restates his thesis that fictitious money creation, unfettered self-interest and interest cause socio-economic problems, then sets an agenda for policymakers internationally and in Muslim states. He reviews the potential of Islamic banking (drivers, fund management, Sukuk, SMEs), the issues it faces (interpretation, integrity, structure, regulation, Shari’ah compliance frameworks) and its challenges (awareness, compliance, competition, benchmarks, innovation, liquidity, people), and concludes with recommendations.","summary":"The prospects for Islamic finance are bright but the task is challenging. Its practice is sustainable and profitable as genuine business, yet it needs changes in procedures, tools and mindsets, collaboration among governments, regulators, industry, academia and Shari’ah scholars, and structural adjustments allowing IFIs to engage in real trading, leasing and real-estate business. Fixity of prices and rentals is not a problem — non-fixity would invalidate them — and IFIs can use securitisation and fund management to offer variable (Shirkah) and quasi-fixed (trade and leasing) returns matched to investors' risk profiles. At the macro level, the author calls for disciplining money creation, diversifying reserve currencies, basing financial assets on real assets, replacing loan-based aid, and phased transformation by Muslim states, especially in public finance. Operations must have positive socio-economic impact — as Zubair Hasan says, Islamic banking is an ongoing process in a milieu of poverty and inequality — supported by education, training, contract enforcement, recovery systems, Shari’ah audits, prudential rules, suitable risk management and accounting standards, and good governance.","summaryExpanded":"The book's concluding chapter sets out a two-level 'way forward' agenda: macro-level policy measures to discipline the global financial system's creation of fictitious monetary assets (diversified reserve currencies, real-asset-backed money/credit creation, replacing loan-based aid), and specific recommendations for developing Islamic finance sustainably. It surveys the industry's real growth drivers and international institutional infrastructure, its substantial potential in fund management, Sukuk and priority sectors like SMEs and microfinance, and works through its major unresolved issues - Shari'ah interpretation (more manageable than perceived), integrity/credibility, institutional structure, regulatory/tax treatment, and Shari'ah compliance framework design (illustrated by Pakistan's actual model). It closes with the industry's core remaining challenges - education, genuine Shari'ah compliance, competitiveness, benchmarks, product innovation, liquidity/monetary management and human resources - before affirming that Islamic banking's prospects are bright but require going beyond the formal abolition of interest to genuine, sustained real-sector engagement.","summarySection":"18.4","takeaways":["Root problem (author): fictitious money creation, self-interest, interest.","International agenda: multiple reserve currencies, asset-matched money, investment instead of loans.","Muslim states should adopt Islamic finance in public finance through OIC/IDB packages.","Ten driving forces behind growth.","Integrity rests on compliance, impact, competence and ethics.","Preferred governance: central Shari’ah board + advisors' forum; SBP Box 18.1.","Seven challenges — awareness is the greatest.","LOLR options: short Mudarabah facility or common pool.","The author's macro-level policy agenda ties reserve currency diversification, real-asset-backed money creation, and reformed foreign aid directly to Islamic finance's underlying principles","Muslim states, with rare exceptions, have not yet adopted Islamic finance as a state-level policy objective, despite the theory's proven viability - a gap the author calls on the OIC/IDB to help close","Ten identified factors (religious demand, scholar involvement, standardization, Gulf wealth, regulatory flexibility, and more) drove Islamic finance's rapid growth into a multi-billion-dollar global industry","The author considers Shari'ah interpretation differences less of a real obstacle than commonly believed, with most major issues already resolved by the OIC Fiqh Council and AAOIFI","Industry credibility depends on four factors: genuine Shari'ah compliance, real socio-economic impact, professional competence, and ethical behaviour by IFI staff","A central Shari'ah board model (coordinating with individual bank advisors), as implemented by the State Bank of Pakistan, is the author's preferred compliance-framework option","IFIs face a structural competitiveness disadvantage from earning no return on statutory liquidity reserves, and the industry still lacks developed inter-bank and lender-of-last-resort mechanisms","The book closes by insisting Islamic finance must go beyond the formal abolition of interest to genuine real-sector engagement, framing the whole project as an ongoing process rather than a finished achievement"],"takeawayOrigins":["f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2"],"checklist":["Can you explain the author's diagnosis of global finance?","Can you list the five international policy measures?","Can you outline a phased transformation plan for Muslim states?","Can you list the ten driving forces?","Can you discuss Shari’ah interpretation and integrity issues?","Can you compare Shari’ah compliance framework options and describe Box 18.1?","Can you list and explain the seven challenges?","Can you describe Islamic lender-of-last-resort options?"],"topics":[{"id":"t18.1","section":"18.1","title":"Introduction: Diagnosis and Agenda","pages":[457,457],"tier":"core","concepts":["fictitious-assets","money-creation"],"intuition":"What does the author believe is the root cause of today's financial problems?","simpleExplanation":"The author believes socio-economic problems stem from unbridled creation of fictitious assets (especially reserve currencies), unhindered market forces driven by individual ‘sovereignty’ and unfettered self-interest, and an interest-based financial system. The solution: discipline money creation, balance self-interest with social interest and ethics, and free finance from exploitation and chance. The chapter gives (1) macro policy measures to phase out fictitious asset creation and (2) ways to develop Islamic finance sustainably.","academicExplanation":["The author cites wars financed by deficit financing and reserve-money creation as an example of the harm of easy fictitious money (e.g. projected Iraq War costs).","The author returns to the diagnosis in the book’s introduction. In his view, today’s socio-economic problems stem from the unbridled creation of fictitious assets (especially reserve currencies), the unchecked forces of demand and supply combined with individual “sovereignty” and “unfettered self-interest”, and an interest-based financial system. He gives war financed by deficit money creation as an example of the damage (p. 457). He proposes three remedies: discipline the creation of money, temper self-interest with social interest and ethics, and free finance from exploitation and games of chance. The chapter has two parts: macro-level policy to phase out fictitious asset creation, and developing a sustainable financial system on Islamic principles built on real assets and business ethics (p. 457)."],"examExplanation":"Diagnosis: fictitious assets + self-interest + interest. Remedy: discipline money creation, ethics, exploitation-free finance. Two-part agenda.","keyPoints":["Links back to the thesis of Chapter 1.","The author presents these as his own views (“I believe”), not as established consensus (p. 457)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Money creation and war","kind":"textbook","text":"The author notes that recent wars financed by deficit money creation cost countless lives; he cites an estimate of about US$1 trillion for the Iraq war. For him this shows the danger of easy creation of fictitious money (p. 457 and footnote)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t1.1","t4.7"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"According to the author, a root cause of global socio-economic problems is:","options":["Too little credit","Unbridled creation of fictitious assets, especially reserve currencies","Excessive equity finance","Too many Islamic banks"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 457."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch18-t1"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch18-t1","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"18.1-18.2","sectionTitle":"Introduction; Agenda for the Policymakers","title":"Macro-Level Policy Agenda: Disciplining Money and Credit Creation","covers":["t18.1","t18.2","t18.2.1"],"overview":"The author frames global socio-economic problems as rooted in unbridled creation of fictitious monetary assets, and proposes macro-level measures - from diversified reserve currencies to replacing loan-based aid - to discipline financial systems on Islamic finance's asset-backed principles.","simpleExplanation":"The author believes many of the world's economic problems come from countries and institutions being able to create money too easily, without it being tied to anything real - so his big-picture fix involves making currencies and financial systems more diverse and more firmly linked to real assets and real economic activity, using Islamic finance's principles as the model, and replacing interest-based loans to poor countries with real investment and asset-based alternatives.","academicExplanation":"The author opens by restating his core thesis (from the book's Introduction): today's socio-economic problems stem from the unbridled creation of fictitious assets - particularly reserve currencies - combined with unhindered demand/supply forces, exploitative 'sovereignty' of individuals, unfettered self-interest, and the interest-based corrupt financial system. He argues massive losses to life and property (citing the Iraq War's roughly $1 trillion cost, financed via deficit financing/money creation) could have been avoided had fictitious monetary asset creation not been so easy and rampant. The chapter's two purposes are: (1) macro-level policy measures to discipline the financial matters of states, corporate bodies and financial institutions, aiming to discontinue fictitious asset creation in phases; and (2) how to develop the financial system sustainably on Islamic finance principles (real tangible assets, business ethics). Section 18.2 argues Islamic finance's principles (risk-related permanent/redeemable capital supply, restricted risk-taking via the Gharar/gambling prohibition, a balanced real-asset-backed return structure, and money supply commensurate with growth prospects) provide a sound basis for sustainable, evenly-shared development - quoting South magazine's 1986 observation that Islamic banking, once dismissed as fundamentalist, is now being adopted pragmatically even by Western institutions. At the international level, the author proposes five measures: (1) increasing the number of reserve currencies for diversification (enhancing the Euro, Japan/China's currencies, and developing regional reserve currencies for South-East Asia, the Far East, Central Asia, the Middle East, Africa, Latin America - with the OIC as the best forum for a widely-acceptable Muslim-world reserve currency), potentially via a Bretton-Woods-style international conference; (2) matching money/credit creation to real sector development and growth potential in all economies (particularly for internationally-used currencies), using Sukuk issuance principles to transform financial asset creation from 'thin air' to real underlying assets; (3) replacing loan-based finance (prone to corrupt-practice squandering) with direct investment in commodity/service sectors, transparent project-based financing, sovereign/multinational portfolio investment to strengthen recipient equity markets, and return-free/in-kind assistance for natural calamities - since interest-based financial aid can be 'fatal' for affected economies; (4) ensuring 'foreign aid' genuinely benefits recipient countries by controlling corrupt commercial behaviour on both donor and recipient sides; (5) projecting Islamic finance as religious/ethical banking appealing to all religions (given Riba's prohibition across revealed religions), mobilizing savings from previously untapped groups toward healthy, real-asset-based investment.","examExplanation":"Core thesis: fictitious (reserve-currency-driven) money creation is the root problem. Five international-level measures: (1) diversify reserve currencies (OIC-led regional currency for Muslim world, Bretton-Woods-style conference); (2) tie money/credit creation to real sector growth potential (Sukuk principles as the model); (3) replace loan-based finance with direct/portfolio investment and return-free calamity assistance; (4) control corrupt behavior in foreign aid on both donor/recipient sides; (5) market Islamic finance as universal religious/ethical banking to mobilize untapped savings.","keyPoints":["The author attributes major socio-economic problems to unbridled creation of fictitious monetary assets, particularly reserve currencies","The chapter has two purposes: macro-level policy discipline for states/institutions, and developing the financial system sustainably on Islamic finance principles","Five proposed international measures: diversifying reserve currencies (with an OIC-led Muslim-world currency), tying money creation to real sector growth, replacing loan-based finance with direct/portfolio investment, controlling corruption in foreign aid, and marketing Islamic finance as universal ethical banking","Sukuk issuance principles are proposed as a model for transforming financial asset creation from 'thin air' to real underlying assets globally","Interest-based financial aid to countries affected by natural calamities is described as potentially 'fatal' for their economies"],"definitions":[],"conditions":[],"principles":["Money and credit creation should be commensurate with real sector development and growth potential in an economy","Loan-based finance should be replaced by direct investment, transparent project financing, portfolio investment, and return-free/in-kind assistance"],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"South Magazine's 1986 Observation","kind":"textbook","text":"South magazine (November 1986) noted that Islamic banking, first ridiculed in the West as a fundamentalist phenomenon and then merely tolerated, was by then being adopted by Western financing institutions and interest-based commercial banks in the Muslim world after careful scrutiny - illustrating the shift toward pragmatic acceptance the author sees continuing."}],"commonConfusions":["Assuming this chapter's 'way forward' is only about Islamic banking product development - the chapter opens with a much broader macro-economic and monetary policy agenda before turning to banking-specific recommendations"],"distinctions":["The chapter's two distinct purposes: macro-level financial system discipline (states, corporate bodies, institutions) versus micro-level development of Islamic finance itself"],"relatedConcepts":["Sukuk","Reserve currencies","Fictitious assets","OIC"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.1-18.2","pages":[457,458,459]}}]},{"id":"t18.2","section":"18.2","title":"Agenda for the Policymakers","pages":[457,459],"tier":"core","concepts":["policy","reserve-currency","money-creation"],"intuition":"What global reforms does the author propose?","simpleExplanation":"Islamic finance offers more risk-related capital, restricted risk-taking (no gambling or Gharar), a balanced return structure based on real assets and money supply matched to growth. Measures: (1) more reserve currencies, including regional ones (the OIC could develop one) and a new Bretton Woods-style conference; (2) money and credit creation matched to real-sector development, using Sukuk principles to base financial assets on real assets; (3) replace loan-based finance with direct investment, transparent project finance, portfolio investment and return-free loans or in-kind aid for calamities; (4) aid that tangibly benefits recipients, controlling corruption; (5) present Islamic finance as religious/ethical banking with appeal to all faiths.","academicExplanation":["South magazine (1986): Islamic banking was first ridiculed, then tolerated, then adopted by Western institutions; the author says the situation has since improved enormously.","Asset-based Islamic banking with self-regulation and ethics may strengthen internal controls and financial soundness, with fewer spill-over effects from excessive money and credit creation.","Green finance and ethical investment movements create demand for asset-based finance; transparency, justice and social responsibility are core Islamic values.","The author argues that Islamic finance offers a sound basis for sustainable development. It supplies more risk-bearing capital, restrains risk-taking through the bans on gambling and Gharar, bases returns on real assets, and ties money supply to growth prospects (pp. 457–458). He cites a 1986 South magazine survey: Islamic banking was first ridiculed in the West, then tolerated, and then adopted after scrutiny. He adds that asset-based banking with rigorous self-regulation should strengthen internal controls and financial soundness (p. 458). Interest in green and ethical finance shows a growing market that Islamic finance, with its core values of transparency, justice and social responsibility, is well placed to serve (p. 458)."],"examExplanation":"Five international measures: multiple reserve currencies (OIC; new Bretton Woods); money creation matched to real assets (Sukuk principles); replace loan-based finance; honest aid; ethical/religious appeal.","keyPoints":["The prohibition of Riba in all revealed religions enhances appeal.","The author suggests a new Bretton Woods-type conference on reserves and currencies, with the OIC as a forum for a widely acceptable reserve currency (pp. 458–459).","For large natural disasters he recommends return-free loans or aid in kind rather than interest-based aid (p. 459)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"From loans to investment in aid","kind":"textbook","text":"Instead of interest-bearing aid, the author proposes direct investment, transparent project finance, portfolio investment by sovereigns, and return-free loans or aid in kind after natural disasters (p. 459)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t18.2.1","t4.7"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which forum does the author suggest could develop a widely acceptable regional reserve currency?","options":["The IMF","The OIC","The WTO","The FSA"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 458."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"processSteps":["Diversify reserve currencies (regional/OIC currency)","Match money and credit creation to real-sector growth","Replace loan-based finance with investment and project finance","Make aid tangible and corruption-free","Promote Islamic finance as ethical banking to all"],"debate":[{"issue":"Can Islamic finance become a model for the global system?","criticism":"The global system rests on fiat reserve currencies and interest-based credit, and changing it would require unprecedented cooperation.","response":"The author proposes phased international steps: multiple reserve currencies, money creation tied to real assets through Sukuk principles, and investment in place of loan-based aid, arguing for their benefits to all societies.","takeaway":"These are the author’s policy recommendations, offered for policymakers to consider (pp. 458–459)."}]},{"id":"t18.2.1","section":"18.2.1","title":"Muslim States and Islamic Finance","pages":[459,461],"tier":"core","concepts":["policy","public-finance","benchmark"],"intuition":"Why does Islamic banking have little macro impact while governments borrow on interest?","simpleExplanation":"Muslim countries, with one or two exceptions, do not promote Islamic finance as state policy and still borrow mainly on interest. The OIC or IDB could design packages obliging members to use Ijarah, Istisna‘a and Shirkah, which would also discipline fiscal behaviour and create real benchmarks. If Islamic finance remains limited to retail and corporate banking while governments borrow conventionally, it will have no visible macro impact. Transformation needs (i) identifying legal, regulatory and institutional requirements, (ii) a phased plan with implementation mechanisms, and (iii) continuous review. A few countries acting alone face exogenous money-supply pressures.","academicExplanation":["Obtaining a benchmark for Shari’ah-compliant securities is almost impossible at present, which is why IFIs use interest-based benchmarks, making their integrity vulnerable.","The conventional system evolved over a long period from gold to electronic money; correcting it will take time and sacrifice.","Muslim states still borrow mostly through interest-based instruments. Only one or two countries treat Islamic finance as a state-level policy objective (pp. 459–460). The author suggests that the OIC or IDB create financing packages obliging members to use asset-based Ijarah, Istisna‘a and Shirkah. This would discipline fiscal behaviour and produce real benchmarks for pricing goods and usufruct, without which IFIs must use interest benchmarks (p. 460). If only retail and corporate banking become Islamic while governments keep borrowing conventionally, there will be no visible impact on economies (p. 460). A country that moves alone may still import excess money supply from major economies. Closer links between exogenous money and state policy on capital flows, investment, fiscal, trade and pricing matters can partly offset this (pp. 460–461)."],"examExplanation":"Muslim states: little policy role; still borrow on interest; OIC/IDB packages (Ijarah, Istisna‘a, Shirkah); real benchmarks; phased transformation (requirements → plan → review).","keyPoints":["Public-sector adoption is key to macro impact."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Why state borrowing matters","kind":"practice","text":"If a country’s banks become Islamic but its government keeps borrowing through interest-bearing bonds, the benchmark rates and money creation of the economy stay interest-based. The author expects little visible impact until public finance also moves to Ijarah, Istisna‘a and Shirkah (p. 460)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Converting retail and corporate banks alone will transform a Muslim economy.","right":"The author argues it will have little visible impact while governments keep financing themselves through interest-based markets (p. 460)."}],"relatedTopics":["t18.3.3","t15.3.8"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why does the author say Islamic finance has limited macro impact at present?","options":["It is too large","Governments continue to raise funds mainly from conventional interest-based markets","It has no products","Central banks prohibit it"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 460."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch18-t2"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch18-t2","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"18.2.1","sectionTitle":"Muslim States and Islamic Finance","title":"Muslim States and Islamic Finance: The Case for State-Level Adoption","covers":["t18.2.1"],"overview":"Despite Islamic finance's proven viability, Muslim states (with rare exceptions) have not adopted it as a policy objective, continuing to borrow conventionally - the author calls for phased, well-planned state-level transformation led by institutions like the OIC/IDB.","simpleExplanation":"Even though Islamic finance has proven it can work, most Muslim-majority governments still borrow money the conventional interest-based way rather than genuinely adopting Islamic finance at the state policy level. The author argues this needs to change through a careful, well-planned, phased transformation - not overnight, but with real commitment - led by institutions like the OIC and the Islamic Development Bank offering financial packages that require member countries to use asset-based financing instead of debt.","academicExplanation":"While Islamic finance principles have proven viable worldwide, and individual IFIs and multinational groups actively pursue its benefits, Muslim countries (with only one or two exceptions) are not yet playing an effective policy-level role in promoting Islamic finance. The foundations - a rational, sound theoretical basis with increasingly wide global acceptance - have been laid; what remains is creating asset-based money and promoting retail/corporate financial services on fair-play and risk-sharing principles. Almost all Islamic countries still borrow through the conventional interest-based system, with only a negligible portion financed via Shari'ah-compliant instruments; the firm Shari'ah prohibition on fixed-income securities rules out conventional investment instruments. The OIC or IDB could formulate financial packages obliging member countries to use asset-based financing (Ijarah, Istisna'a, Shirkah), which - if applied wholeheartedly at scale - could efficiently replace interest-based deficit financing while disciplining fiscal behaviour; this would also generate genuine benchmarks reflecting real demand/supply and economic strength, addressing the currently 'almost impossible' task of obtaining Shari'ah-compliant benchmarks (forcing IFIs to use interest-based benchmarks everywhere, which the author notes makes their operational integrity vulnerable). If Islamic finance theory is adopted only for retail/corporate banking on some scale, while governments continue primarily generating funds from conventional markets, it won't have visible impact on economies or populations - so economists/policymakers must confidently move away from the current 'syndrome' of unlimited monetary asset creation with fixed rent, toward real-economic-activity-based regimes with ex post profit/loss sharing and fixed/quasi-fixed returns from real goods/asset pricing. The author acknowledges this requires bold but not unprecedented measures already being taken in various economies. Since the conventional financial system developed through a long, continuous process (gold to electronic exchange), correcting it will also take time, requiring sincerity, work and sacrifice by present generations. Full economy-wide transformation requires: (i) identifying transformation requirements (laws, rules, regulations, institutional structures) fitting an overall implementation timetable; (ii) laying down a phased plan and implementation mechanism; and (iii) continuous feedback/review to ensure proper application and remove obstacles. A caveat: if only a few countries apply a real-asset-based system while most major economies continue current money-creation practices, this could cause critical problems for the early adopters due to exogenous factors affecting money supply - partially manageable through closer state linkages between exogenous money and fiscal/trade/pricing policy.","examExplanation":"Key facts: almost all Islamic countries still borrow conventionally; only 'one or two' exceptions show effective state-level Islamic finance promotion. Proposed mechanism: OIC/IDB financial packages OBLIGING member countries toward Ijarah/Istisna'a/Shirkah-based financing. Three-part transformation plan: (i) identify legal/regulatory/institutional requirements, (ii) phased implementation plan, (iii) continuous feedback/review. Caveat: early-adopter countries risk exogenous money-supply problems if most major economies don't also transform.","keyPoints":["Almost all Islamic countries still borrow through the conventional interest-based system, with only a negligible portion financed via Shari'ah-compliant instruments","The OIC or IDB could formulate financial packages obliging member countries to use asset-based financing (Ijarah, Istisna'a, Shirkah)","State-level adoption would also help generate genuine, real-economy-reflective benchmarks - addressing the currently near-impossible task of finding Shari'ah-compliant benchmarks","Full economy-wide transformation requires: identifying legal/regulatory/institutional requirements, a phased implementation plan, and continuous feedback/review","A caveat: if only a few countries adopt a real-asset-based system while most major economies continue current money-creation practices, early adopters risk exogenous money-supply problems"],"definitions":[],"conditions":[],"principles":["Adopting Islamic finance theory only for retail/corporate banking, while governments continue funding primarily from conventional markets, will not produce visible economic or population-wide impact"],"processSteps":["Identify transformation requirements: laws, rules, regulations and institutional structures across jurisdictions","Lay down a phased plan and implementation mechanism","Establish feedback/continuous review and evaluation to ensure proper application and remove obstacles"],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Assuming Islamic finance's theoretical soundness alone guarantees state-level adoption - the author is explicit that Muslim states, with rare exceptions, have not yet made it a policy objective despite proven viability"],"distinctions":["Islamic finance adopted only at the retail/corporate banking level (limited impact) versus adopted as a genuine state/fiscal policy objective (the author's recommended path)"],"relatedConcepts":["OIC","Islamic Development Bank (IDB)","Ijarah","Istisna'a","Shirkah","Benchmarks"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2.1","pages":[459,460,461]}}]},{"id":"t18.3","section":"18.3","title":"Potential, Issues and Challenges: Driving Forces","pages":[461,463],"tier":"core","concepts":["growth-drivers"],"intuition":"What has propelled Islamic finance from niche to global industry?","simpleExplanation":"Islamic finance has grown into a multi-billion-dollar industry with hundreds of institutions and products from Citigroup, Deutsche Bank, ABN AMRO, HSBC and UBS. Ten driving forces: (1) demand for Riba-free investment; (2) resurgence of Muslim cultural values; (3) Shari’ah scholars' active involvement; (4) compliant products for excess liquidity; (5) AAOIFI and IFSB standards; (6) Gulf surplus funds; (7) regulatory flexibility (e.g. US after 1999 Glass–Steagall repeal; UK FSA stamp duty changes); (8) conversion of sound institutions; (9) high net worth investors shifting; (10) the real-asset, ethical nature of products.","academicExplanation":["Support institutions: IDB, IFSB, IIFM, GCIBAFI, ARCIFI (arbitration, Dubai), AAOIFI, IIRA, LMC; Bahrain as the Middle East hub.","Innovations: credit and debit cards; NCB Saudi prepaid ‘advance card’; multi-manager funds; Islamic indices (Dow Jones Islamic, Al-Meezan, Malaysian); Sukuk even in Japan and China.","Islamic finance has grown from a niche into a multi-billion-dollar industry with hundreds of institutions. Western groups such as Citigroup, Deutsche Bank, ABN AMRO, HSBC and UBS now offer Shari’ah-based products (p. 461). Successes include product innovation, venture capital, equity, trade, housing and consumer finance, and large project and infrastructure deals, mainly in the Middle East (p. 461). There are credit and debit cards, and NCB Saudi Arabia offers a prepaid “advance card”. There are multi-manager funds and Islamic indices (Dow Jones Islamic, Al-Meezan, Malaysian), and Sukuk have been issued even in Japan and China (pp. 461–462). The support infrastructure includes IDB, IFSB, IIFM, GCIBAFI, ARCIFI (dispute resolution, Dubai), AAOIFI, IIRA and LMC. Bahrain is the main Middle East hub (p. 462)."],"examExplanation":"Ten drivers; support institutions (IDB, IFSB, IIFM, AAOIFI, IIRA, LMC, ARCIFI, GCIBAFI); innovations (cards, indices, Sukuk).","keyPoints":["Standards (AAOIFI, IFSB) enhance credibility."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Driving force: regulatory flexibility","kind":"textbook","text":"After the US repealed the Glass–Steagall Act in 1999, more than two dozen Islamic investment institutions began serving Riba-averse communities. The UK’s FSA eased the treatment of Islamic deposits and single stamp duty on mortgages (pp. 462–463)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t18.3.1","t8.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which US legal change does the author link to growth of Islamic investment institutions in the USA?","options":["Dodd–Frank","Repeal of the Glass–Steagall Act in 1999","Sarbanes–Oxley","Basel II"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 462."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch18-t3"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch18-t3","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"18.3","sectionTitle":"Potential, Issues and Challenges for Islamic Banking","title":"Islamic Banking's Growth Drivers and Global Footprint","covers":["t18.3","t18.3.1","t18.3.2","t18.3.2.b","t18.3.2.c","t18.3.3"],"overview":"Islamic finance has grown from a niche market into a multi-billion-dollar global industry, driven by ten identifiable factors ranging from religious demand to regulatory flexibility, supported by a growing international institutional infrastructure.","simpleExplanation":"Islamic finance has grown remarkably - not just in Muslim countries but with major Western banks now offering Shari'ah-compliant products too - thanks to a combination of factors: genuine religious demand, cultural resurgence, active scholarly involvement, growing product sophistication, industry standardization, oil-driven wealth in the Gulf, and friendlier regulation in places like the UK and US. A whole ecosystem of supporting institutions (rating agencies, standard-setters, dispute-resolution bodies) has also grown up around the industry.","academicExplanation":"Islamic finance has rapidly grown from a specialized niche market into a multi-billion-dollar global industry, with continued double-digit growth expected. Hundreds of Islamic retail banks/NBFIs now operate in both Muslim-majority and Muslim-minority countries, with significantly grown product portfolios; major Western financial institutions (Citigroup, Deutsche Bank, ABN AMRO, HSBC, UBS) increasingly offer Shari'ah-compliant products. Islamic banks in Kuwait, Bahrain, Saudi Arabia, Sudan, Britain and elsewhere show tangible success in product innovation, venture capital, equity finance, trade/housing/consumer finance, and international syndication for trade and project finance - with growing project finance agreements (power stations, water plants, roads, bridges) especially in the Middle East. Electronic money has expanded (Kuwait Finance House, Dubai Islamic Bank credit cards; widespread debit cards; National Commercial Bank of Saudi Arabia's prepaid 'advance card' attractive to women, the young, self-employed and small-business employees who may not meet strict conventional credit card requirements). Efficient Shari'ah-compliant vehicles have developed: general and multimanager Islamic funds, and Islamic market indices with screening/purification criteria (Dow Jones Islamic Index, Al-Meezan Islamic Investment Index, the Malaysian Islamic Index); Sukuk have been successfully introduced even in Japan and China. International infrastructure institutions playing crucial promotion/standardization roles include the IDB, IFSB, IIFM, GCIBAFI, ARCIFI (dispute resolution, based in Dubai), AAOIFI and various Shari'ah councils, plus commercial support institutions like the IIRA and LMC; Bahrain serves as the main Middle East hub. Ten identified growth-driving factors: (1) increasing demand for Riba-free instruments for religious reasons; (2) resurgence of Muslim cultural values; (3) active Shari'ah scholar involvement with bankers/practitioners improving product availability and awareness; (4) development of Shari'ah-compliant investment products, deploying IFIs' early excess liquidity and broadening the Islamic capital market; (5) standardization of modes/products and accounting/auditing procedures (especially AAOIFI's Shari'ah Standards), plus IFSB risk management/capital adequacy/corporate governance standards; (6) growth of Gulf region surplus money from rising petroleum prices and Muslim-country economic growth; (7) regulatory flexibility, deregulation and privatization (e.g. the US's 1999 repeal of the Glass-Steagall Act of 1933 enabling over two dozen US Islamic investment institutions; the UK FSA's flexible treatment of IFI deposits and one-time-only stamp duty on mortgages, boosting UK and broader European Islamic finance); (8) conversion of some financially sound Middle Eastern institutions to Islamic modes, providing self-enforcing momentum; (9) high-net-worth individuals shifting to Shari'ah-compliant institutions as awareness grows; (10) the real-asset-based, ethical nature of Islamic products (avoiding gambling and interest) increasing demand and widening the customer base.","examExplanation":"Ten growth-driving factors (know at least half): religious demand, cultural resurgence, scholar-banker collaboration, product development/liquidity deployment, standardization (AAOIFI/IFSB), Gulf petrodollar surplus, regulatory flexibility (US Glass-Steagall repeal 1999, UK FSA flexibility), conversion of sound institutions, high-net-worth investor shift, real-asset/ethical appeal. Key institutions: IDB, IFSB, IIFM, GCIBAFI, ARCIFI (Dubai, dispute resolution), AAOIFI, IIRA, LMC (Bahrain hub).","keyPoints":["Major Western financial institutions (Citigroup, Deutsche Bank, ABN AMRO, HSBC, UBS) increasingly offer Shari'ah-compliant products","The US's 1999 repeal of the Glass-Steagall Act of 1933 enabled the growth of over two dozen US Islamic investment institutions","The UK FSA's flexible treatment of IFI deposits and mortgage stamp duty gave a major impetus to Islamic finance in the UK and broader Europe","Ten identified factors drive Islamic finance growth, spanning religious demand, scholar involvement, product/standardization development, Gulf petrodollar wealth, and regulatory flexibility","Key international institutions include the IDB, IFSB, IIFM, GCIBAFI, AAOIFI, IIRA and LMC (Bahrain), plus ARCIFI in Dubai for dispute resolution","Islamic market indices with screening/purification criteria include the Dow Jones Islamic Index, Al-Meezan Islamic Investment Index and the Malaysian Islamic Index"],"definitions":[{"term":"ARCIFI","definition":"The International Arbitration and Reconciliation Centre for Islamic Financial Institutions, based in Dubai, working to resolve disputes between industry participants and their counterparties."}],"conditions":[],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"National Commercial Bank of Saudi Arabia's Advance Card","kind":"textbook","text":"This prepaid (not credit-line-based) card offers purchase protection and travel accident insurance without interest or extra fees, making it especially attractive to women, the young, the self-employed and small establishment employees who may not meet strict conventional credit card requirements."}],"commonConfusions":["Assuming Islamic finance growth has been confined to Muslim-majority countries - major Western institutions and regulatory changes in the US and UK have been significant growth drivers too"],"distinctions":["Shari'ah standard-setting/support institutions (AAOIFI, IFSB) versus dispute-resolution/rating institutions (ARCIFI, IIRA, LMC) - both categories support the industry but serve different functions"],"relatedConcepts":["AAOIFI","IFSB","IIFM","Sukuk","Islamic indices"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3","pages":[461,462,463]}}],"table":{"caption":"Ten driving forces of Islamic finance (pp. 462–463)","head":["#","Driving force"],"rows":[["1","Religious demand for Riba-free investment"],["2","Resurgence of Muslim cultural values"],["3","Scholars working with bankers"],["4","Compliant products absorbing excess liquidity"],["5","Standardisation (AAOIFI, IFSB)"],["6","Gulf oil surpluses and economic growth"],["7","Regulatory flexibility (US after Glass–Steagall repeal, 1999; UK FSA)"],["8","Conversion of sound Middle East institutions"],["9","High-net-worth investors shifting"],["10","Real-asset-based, ethical nature of products"]]}},{"id":"t18.3.1","section":"18.3.1","title":"Promising Potential: Funds, Sukuk and Specific Sectors","pages":[463,465],"tier":"core","concepts":["fund-management","sukuk","microfinance"],"intuition":"Where is the untapped growth for Islamic banks?","simpleExplanation":"Potential should be judged against the pioneers' objectives. Fund management: low-risk funds from short-term Murabaha and leasing for risk-averse savers; high-return funds from Musharakah and long-term leasing; restricted investment accounts; equity funds; domestic and international trade funds for SMEs and trade (Grameen and Bangladesh Islamic Bank experience). Sukuk: liquidity, monetary policy, infrastructure — but reliance on Ijarah Sukuk alone is not enough; Shirkah-based Sukuk needed. Specific sectors: small business, cottage industry, agriculture; Islamic microfinance institutions with fewer default and moral-hazard problems, using Zakat and Waqf funds. High net worth portfolios with banks as Mudarib or agent.","academicExplanation":["Structural adjustments are needed to deal with real-sector trading, leasing and real-estate contracts.","Habib Ahmed (IDB) has studied problems and prospects of Islamic microfinance institutions.","Since the foundations are laid, the author says potential should now be judged against the pioneers’ objectives and the social impact watched by contemporary scholars (p. 463). He sees large potential in retail, corporate and investment banking, fund management and Sukuk. Realising it needs structural adjustments that let IFIs deal in the real sector through trade, leasing and real estate (p. 463). Funds could include low-variation funds based on short-term Murabaha and leasing for risk-averse savers, and higher-return Musharakah or long-lease funds. Restricted investment accounts would give equity exposure to specific projects, and there could be open-ended equity funds and local or foreign currency trade funds for SMEs and trade (p. 464). He also points to SMEs, cottage industry, agriculture and microfinance. Islamic MFIs may face lower default, moral hazard and information-asymmetry problems and can draw on Zakat and Waqf. He cites Habib Ahmed’s IDB study (p. 465)."],"examExplanation":"Potential: fund management (low-risk vs high-return funds, trade funds), Sukuk (Shirkah-based needed), SMEs/agriculture/microfinance (Zakat, Waqf), HNWI portfolios.","keyPoints":["IMFIs may have lower default and moral hazard.","For high-net-worth clients, banks can act as Mudarib (profit share) or agent (fee) in individual portfolios (p. 465)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Grameen and Bangladesh Islamic Bank","kind":"textbook","text":"Their experience suggests low-risk trade funds can be invested mainly in small and medium business and trading, where default is expected to be lowest (p. 464)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t15.3.8","t12.7.3"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which fund type does the author suggest for risk-averse savers?","options":["Musharakah venture funds","Funds based on short-term Murabaha and leasing","Derivative funds","Equity growth funds"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 464."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch18-t4"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch18-t4","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"18.3.1","sectionTitle":"Promising Potential","title":"Promising Potential: Fund Management, Sukuk and Specific Sectors","covers":["t18.3.1"],"overview":"The author identifies major untapped potential in Islamic fund management (risk-tiered fund categories), Sukuk-based capital markets, and priority sectors like SMEs, microfinance and agriculture.","simpleExplanation":"The author sees huge room for growth in a few specific areas: offering different types of Islamic investment funds for different risk appetites (safe short-term Murabaha/leasing funds versus higher-return Musharakah funds), expanding Sukuk beyond over-reliance on Ijarah, and specifically targeting small businesses, microfinance and agriculture - sectors where Islamic finance's real-asset-based approach could be especially effective at reducing poverty and unemployment.","academicExplanation":"Islamic banking/finance has large potential in retail, corporate and investment banking and fund management; the requirement that transactions be based on fixed assets, services or asset/service-representing instruments makes investment banking, fund management and Sukuk issuance particularly lucrative alongside retail/corporate banking. Realizing this requires structural adjustments enabling IFIs to deal with real sector business, implementing trading/leasing/real-estate contracts using both participatory and nonparticipatory modes - necessary both to consolidate current success and to realize the fund management/securitization potential, providing Halal, relatively better investor returns. Potential in Fund Management: IFIs may engage in fund/portfolio management through regulated asset management companies, broadly in two categories: (1) minimum-variation-return funds based on short-term Murabaha and local/foreign currency leasing operations, earmarked for goods purchase/resale on mark-up and short/medium-term leasing giving fixed earnings, suited to risk-averse savers unable to afford PLS-based losses; (2) high-return funds based on Musharakah or long-term leasing for those willing to take risk. Banks may also offer restricted/specific investment accounts (equity exposure via identified investment opportunities offered to existing account holders, who share the PROJECT's profits rather than the bank's), open-ended multiple equity funds invested in stocks, and domestic-currency trade funds for minimal-risk savers (financing SMEs and domestic/international trade - the Grameen Bank of Bangladesh and Bangladesh Islamic Bank experiences show such funds can achieve the lowest default levels/ratios in small/medium-level business), plus international/foreign-currency trade funds financing client imports/exports. Potential Relating to Sukuk: Sukuk have confirmed viability in resource mobilization for both investors and fund users, with potential as a monetary policy/open market operations and liquidity management tool. They create a large-scale public/private-sector project participation framework (infrastructure: roads, bridges, ports, airports), requiring appropriate enabling laws, accounting standards, targeted market study, standardized contract monitoring, financial data flow to investors, and standard-quality customer service. International institutions (Bahrain-based LMC, IIFM, IIRA) must do more to make Sukuk/fund management/Islamic capital markets active and efficient, and must push industry players to exploit Shirkah-based Sukuk potential, since continued sole reliance on Ijarah Sukuk (as witnessed over the last five years at the time of writing) may not realize the industry's full securitization potential. Potential in Specific Sectors: asset-based Islamic finance modes suit enhancing business/capital formation in priority sectors - small business, cottage industry and agriculture - which could be highly profitable for IFIs while benefiting business/industrial communities and economies, since SME financing and microfinance have proven effective for broad-based growth and poverty alleviation in developing/emerging economies. Islamic microfinance institutions (IMFIs) would have fewer default, moral hazard and asymmetric information problems (citing Habib Ahmed's IDB study on IMFI prospects/problems). Enhancing the financial sector's role in micro-level business could mitigate unemployment and low export levels in developing countries; the post-investment role required of Islamic financiers (versus pure lenders) could substantially benefit SME/economic development if a full-fledged financial-help structure (industry-specific and generalist investment models) is provided. IMFIs can also draw on Zakat and Waqf institutional income sources for direct needy small-business financing without middlemen. For high-net-worth/big investors, banks may offer individual investment portfolios and investment funds in Shirkah-based and debt-creating modes per risk profile, with banks serving as Mudarib (earning a predetermined profit-ratio share) or as agent (earning a fee, with the rest going to fund owners).","examExplanation":"Two broad fund categories: (1) minimum-variation-return funds (short-term Murabaha/leasing, fixed earnings, suited to risk-averse savers); (2) high-return funds (Musharakah/long-term leasing, for risk-tolerant investors). Sukuk potential requires: enabling laws, accounting standards, market study, standardized contracts, investor data flow, quality service - plus more Shirkah-based Sukuk (not just Ijarah). Specific-sector potential: SME/microfinance/agriculture financing shows FEWER default/moral hazard/asymmetric information problems in Islamic structures, per Habib Ahmed's IDB study; Grameen Bank/Bangladesh Islamic Bank experience cited as evidence.","keyPoints":["Islamic fund management can be tiered into minimum-variation-return funds (short-term Murabaha/leasing, for risk-averse savers) and high-return funds (Musharakah/long-term leasing, for risk-tolerant investors)","Restricted/specific investment accounts let banks offer equity exposure where investors share a specific project's profits, not the bank's own profits","Grameen Bank of Bangladesh and Bangladesh Islamic Bank's experience shows SME/trade-focused funds can achieve the lowest default levels/ratios","Sukuk's full potential requires appropriate enabling laws, accounting standards, market study, standardized contract monitoring, investor data flow and quality service - plus reduced reliance on Ijarah Sukuk alone","Islamic microfinance institutions (IMFIs) are expected to have fewer default, moral hazard and asymmetric information problems than conventional microfinance","IMFIs can draw on Zakat and Waqf institutional income sources to directly finance needy small-business enterprises without a middleman"],"definitions":[{"term":"Restricted/Specific Investment Account","definition":"A bank account structure where the bank identifies a specific investment opportunity and invites account holders to subscribe, with investors sharing the profits of that particular project rather than the bank's overall profit."}],"conditions":[],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Grameen Bank and Bangladesh Islamic Bank Experience","kind":"textbook","text":"Experience from Grameen Bank of Bangladesh and the Bangladesh Islamic Bank reveals that banks investing domestic currency trade funds mainly in small- and medium-level business and trading activities can achieve the lowest expected default level/ratio, supporting the case for expanded SME- and trade-focused Islamic fund products."}],"commonConfusions":["Assuming all Islamic investment funds carry the same risk/return profile - the chapter explicitly distinguishes minimum-variation-return funds (Murabaha/leasing-based) from high-return funds (Musharakah/long-term leasing-based)"],"distinctions":["A restricted/specific investment account (investor shares a specific project's profit) versus a general investment deposit (investor shares the bank's overall pool profit)"],"relatedConcepts":["Murabaha","Musharakah","Sukuk","Islamic microfinance","Zakat","Waqf"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.1","pages":[463,464,465]}}]},{"id":"t18.3.2","section":"18.3.2","title":"Issues: Shari’ah Interpretation and Integrity/Credibility","pages":[465,469],"tier":"core","concepts":["ijtihad","credibility","ethics"],"intuition":"Is ‘difference of opinion’ really the main obstacle?","simpleExplanation":"Shari’ah interpretation is no longer as big an issue as claimed: the OIC Fiqh Council and AAOIFI have provided alternatives to most products except interest-based derivatives, short-selling and speculative FX; innovation need not copy every conventional product. Remaining issues are practical (e.g. liquidated damages for default — courts might consider the client's position and litigation costs rather than opportunity cost; shareholders' liability to investment account holders). Integrity depends on: Shari’ah compliance of products; impact on clients and society; professional competence; ethical conduct of staff. Religious leaders need education (AAOIFI standards); staff need training on philosophy; codes of conduct (AAOIFI Code of Ethics); fixed returns paid by shifting shareholders' profit without disclosure are against the spirit of Shari’ah.","academicExplanation":["Practitioners in the Far East have started using Ijarah for Sukuk and are expected to move away from debt sale and buy-back.","PLS modes provide risk capital for projects, imports, pre-shipment exports, working capital and single transactions; non-PLS modes complement them — Murabaha for risk-averse investors, leasing for fixed assets, Salam for agriculture (a Salam-based forward commodity market for rural development).","Competition with mainstream banks and shared benchmarks mean visible socio-economic results will take longer."],"examExplanation":"Interpretation largely resolved (OIC, AAOIFI); remaining practical issues (liquidated damages, shareholder liability). Integrity factors (4). Remedies: educate religious leaders, train staff, codes of ethics, disclosure of profit smoothing.","keyPoints":["Stick to AAOIFI standards to avoid inconsistency.","Profit equalisation allowed with disclosure; secret apportioning is not.","Religious leaders who say no “bank” can be Islamic should be educated, at least in the AAOIFI Shari’ah Standards (p. 467).","Salam could support a non-speculative forward commodity market for rural development (p. 468).","IFIs should adopt a code of conduct reflecting Islamic values (see the AAOIFI Code of Ethics) (p. 468)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Liquidated damages for persistent default","kind":"textbook","text":"The penalty normally goes to charity. One solution is claiming loss through a court or arbitration, deciding on the client’s finances, behaviour and litigation cost rather than opportunity cost. Some boards allow a rate based on the income earned on a similar portfolio (p. 466)."},{"title":"Smoothing returns without disclosure","kind":"textbook","text":"Some IFIs pay fixed-looking returns from the shareholders’ share, or allocate more to shareholders when profits are high. Profit equalisation reserves are fine with disclosure, but apportioning profit secretly to compete is against the spirit of the Shari’ah (p. 469)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Differences of Shari’ah interpretation remain the main obstacle to Islamic finance.","right":"The author holds that the major early issues have been resolved by the OIC Fiqh Council, AAOIFI and scholars. What remains stems mostly from practical difficulties (pp. 465–466)."},{"wrong":"Product innovation means creating an Islamic version of every conventional product.","right":"That is neither possible nor desirable; an ethical discipline cannot follow an unethical system step by step. Interest-based derivatives, short-selling and speculative FX have no alternatives (p. 466)."}],"relatedTopics":["t17.3.6","t14.3.4"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"For which products does the author say Shari’ah scholars have provided no alternatives?","options":["Leasing and trade finance","Interest-based derivatives, short-selling and speculative FX transactions","Housing finance","Deposits"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 466."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch18-t5"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch18-t5","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"18.3.2-A","sectionTitle":"Issues in Islamic Finance: Shari'ah Interpretation and Integrity/Credibility","title":"Shari'ah Interpretation as an Overstated Issue, and the Real Challenge of Industry Credibility","covers":["t18.3.2","t18.3.2.b","t18.3.2.c"],"overview":"The author argues Shari'ah interpretation differences are less of a real obstacle than commonly believed, while identifying integrity and credibility (grounded in genuine Shari'ah compliance, real socio-economic impact, professional competence, and ethical behaviour) as the more substantive ongoing issue.","simpleExplanation":"Many people think disagreement among scholars is the biggest problem holding Islamic finance back, but the author argues that's overstated - major issues have mostly been resolved, and the few genuinely unresolved cases (like exactly how much a bank can charge a defaulter for real losses) can be handled case-by-case by qualified scholars. The bigger real problem is credibility: many people - including religious leaders and even people working inside Islamic banks - simply don't trust or fully understand whether Islamic banks' products are genuinely Shari'ah-compliant, so the industry needs serious investment in education and transparent, ethical practice to earn that trust.","academicExplanation":"The author poses the question directly: is differing Shari'ah interpretation really a major issue, 'or merely a buzz word'? He answers that it is not as big an issue as commonly considered, no longer a real problem in most respects - the OIC's Islamic Fiqh Council, AAOIFI and Shari'ah scholars generally have already discussed almost all major areas at length, providing alternatives to most conventional products (except interest-based derivatives, short-selling and speculative Forex transactions); practitioners should now proceed on the achieved consensus, understanding that Islamic finance need not replicate every conventional instrument (an ethics-based discipline cannot follow an unethical system step by step, or it becomes exploitative/unjust like its counterpart). Some genuinely unresolved issues DO exist, but these stem from practical difficulties/ground realities rather than pure Shari'ah interpretation gaps, and can be resolved by bodies like the OIC Islamic Fiqh Council or AAOIFI - or, failing that, by individual banks' Shari'ah boards/advisors via Ijtihad within accepted Shari'ah principles. Examples of such unresolved issues: (1) the award of liquidated damages to banks on defaulted receivables - in principle the penalty goes to charity, but persistent long-term default is harmful/unjust to the bank and depositors; one accepted approach lets banks claim actual loss through a court/independent arbitration, though the basis courts should use for deciding remains unsettled (conventional opportunity cost per se should NOT be the basis; some Shari'ah boards allow charging a rate based on the bank's income from a similar portfolio in the relevant period, with the court weighing the client's financial position/behaviour and litigation expenses/time); (2) the liability of Islamic banks' shareholders toward investment account holders - a complicated conflict-of-interest issue requiring a firm regulatory framework specifically safeguarding depositor interests, since losses could stem from overexposure, lack of diversification, imprudent practices, or genuine business losses, with bank Shari'ah boards deciding cases on merit. Encouragingly, practitioners in the Far East have started using genuine interest alternatives (like Ijarah) for Sukuk issuance, with anticipated future movement away from sale-of-debt/buy-back bases; going forward, policymakers/developers should focus on developing products using ALREADY-AVAILABLE flexibilities rather than wasting time/money on areas that would only cost the discipline's integrity, sticking to AAOIFI standards (based on deep Fiqh Academy research) to avoid inconsistent interpretation across scholars. On Integrity and Credibility: this has become a genuine issue, with many people (intentionally or not) doubting not just the system's credibility but the intention/integrity of the scholars and practitioners driving its evolution - requiring joint effort from Islamic banks, central banks/regulators and Shari'ah scholars for rapid growth. The author identifies FOUR factors determining IFI integrity: (1) the status of Shari'ah compliance of their products; (2) the impact of products on clients/society/economy; (3) professional competence and care for stakeholder interests; (4) IFI incumbents' behaviour toward and observance of Shari'ah norms. On factor 1: Shari'ah scholars play a crucial role, but many religious leaders don't fully understand IFI operations, issuing edicts prohibiting investment in ANY institution called a 'bank' regardless of actual operations - requiring IFI associations/central banks to launch awareness/education programmes for such leaders, introducing at minimum the AAOIFI Shari'ah Standards in detail. On factor 2: Islamic bankers often fail to articulate their real socio-economic impact versus conventional banks, since (like conventional bankers) they're simply trained to earn profit, without being given the broader socio-economic framing - policymakers/product developers must keep socio-economic considerations alongside profit considerations. On factors 3-4: the moral dimension is central to Islamic banking; IFIs should implement a code of conduct (per the AAOIFI's Code of Ethics) reflecting Islamic values in management, operations and overall staff behaviour, since any unethical/prohibited practice by an Islamic banker undermines the whole system's integrity - the goal being to balance shareholder and depositor interests, avoiding the observed practice of some IFIs giving fixed competitive returns (funded from shareholders' share or reallocated higher-profit periods) purely to compete, which - while profit equalization reserves are legitimate with proper disclosure - is against the Shari'ah's spirit when done without taking partners into confidence.","examExplanation":"Author's verdict: Shari'ah interpretation is 'not as big an issue as some people consider' - major areas resolved by OIC Fiqh Council/AAOIFI; remaining unresolved issues (e.g. liquidated damages basis, shareholder liability to investment account holders) are practical/ground-reality issues, resolvable by individual bank Shari'ah boards via Ijtihad. Integrity's FOUR determinants: (1) product Shari'ah-compliance status, (2) impact on clients/society, (3) professional competence/stakeholder care, (4) incumbents' Shari'ah-norm behaviour. Recommended fix for factor 1: education programmes for religious leaders, introducing AAOIFI Shari'ah Standards.","keyPoints":["The author argues Shari'ah interpretation is 'not as big an issue as some people consider' - major areas have already been resolved by the OIC Islamic Fiqh Council and AAOIFI","Genuinely unresolved issues (e.g. the basis for awarding liquidated damages on defaulted receivables) stem from practical difficulties, not pure Shari'ah interpretation gaps, and are resolvable case-by-case","Conventional opportunity cost per se should not be the basis for awarding liquidated damages; some Shari'ah boards use the bank's income from a similar portfolio instead","IFI integrity depends on four factors: Shari'ah compliance status of products, real socio-economic impact, professional competence/stakeholder care, and incumbents' ethical behaviour","Many religious leaders lack full understanding of IFI operations and issue blanket edicts against any institution called a 'bank' - requiring targeted education programmes","IFIs giving fixed competitive returns purely to compete (funded from shareholders' share, without taking depositor-partners into confidence) is against the spirit of Shari'ah, even though disclosed profit equalization reserves are legitimate"],"definitions":[],"conditions":[],"principles":["Conventional opportunity cost per se should not be the basis for a court-awarded liquidated damages penalty against a defaulting client"],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Assuming Shari'ah interpretation differences are the single biggest obstacle to Islamic finance's growth - the author argues credibility/integrity (a communication, ethics and professional-competence issue) is the more substantive ongoing challenge"],"distinctions":["Genuine Shari'ah interpretation gaps (largely resolved, per the author) versus practical/ground-reality issues that merely LOOK like interpretation disputes (still being worked out case-by-case)"],"relatedConcepts":["OIC Islamic Fiqh Council","AAOIFI","Ijtihad","Code of Ethics","Profit equalization reserve"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[465,466,467,468,469]}}],"table":{"caption":"Factors on which the integrity of IFIs depends (Section 18.3.2)","head":["#","Factor","Suggested action"],"rows":[["1","Shari’ah compliance of products","Educate religious leaders; introduce AAOIFI standards"],["2","Impact on clients, society and economy","Train bankers in philosophy; weigh socio-economic considerations"],["3","Professional competence and care for stakeholders","Best services at competitive rates; balance shareholders and depositors"],["4","Ethical behaviour of incumbents","Code of conduct (AAOIFI Code of Ethics); good governance"]]}},{"id":"t18.3.2.b","section":"18.3.2","title":"Issues: Structure of IFIs and Regulatory/Tax Framework","pages":[469,472],"tier":"core","concepts":["regulation","tax","universal-banking"],"intuition":"What must regulators change so Islamic banks can compete fairly?","simpleExplanation":"Structure: IFIs are intermediaries that must deal in goods, not money, and need not hold inventory (AAOIFI's Murabaha to Purchase Orderer). The concern that they focus on short-term commercial finance is genuine; they should diversify into project and infrastructure finance, corporate advisory and Sukuk, possibly via asset management, leasing and trading subsidiaries. Regulation is needed for information, saver protection, compliance, a legal framework without cost-of-funds recovery, and monetary policy. Tax on purchases creates an uneven playing field, pushing banks to questionable practices; the UK FSA's flexibilities are welcome. Ten regulatory steps include avoiding dual taxation, compliance frameworks, fair profit distribution, disclosure, prudential rules, keeping current-account risk off investment accounts, staff training, research centres and rating institutions.","academicExplanation":["Liberalised regulators rarely intervene in rate structures; spreads have widened instead of passing income to depositors — IFI depositors need more vigilance as they bear extra risk.","Distinguish wilful defaulters (heavy charges as deterrent) from genuine hardship (respite without charge), with restructuring schemes.","Regulators may enlist qualified Shari’ah advisors, train scholars in finance, regulate transparent disposal of charity funds (e.g. grants or Qard to poor students, widows, the sick) and discourage lavish marketing."],"examExplanation":"Structure: goods not money; diversify; subsidiaries. Regulation reasons (5) and steps (10). Tax neutrality (UK FSA). Default treatment. Charity fund rules.","keyPoints":["Current-account risk should not pass to investment accounts.","IFIs should diversify into project and infrastructure finance, Sukuk advisory and restructuring, with subsidiaries for trading and leasing (pp. 469–470).","Regulators should ensure current-account risks stay with the bank and are not passed to investment accounts, especially in distress (p. 471).","Charity funds could be used for grants or return-free loans to students, widows and the sick, and banks should prefer community projects to lavish marketing (pp. 471–472).","The author says liberalised regulators rarely intervene in rates although spreads widen; depositors of IFIs, who bear more risk, need closer vigilance (p. 471)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Double tax on Murabaha","kind":"textbook","text":"A bank buys a house and sells it to a client by Murabaha. Stamp duty on both transfers makes the Islamic product dearer than a mortgage. The UK’s relief — duty paid only once — is the kind of flexibility the author asks regulators for (pp. 462–463, 470)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Islamic banks must hold inventory like traders to be genuine.","right":"They buy on the client’s requisition for sale or lease. There is no Shari’ah objection, hence the AAOIFI Standard is titled Murabaha to Purchase Orderer (p. 469)."},{"wrong":"Taxes on Islamic banks’ purchases are neutral.","right":"Taxing their asset purchases creates an uneven playing field and double taxation. It pushes some banks into doubtful practices to avoid the costs (p. 470)."}],"relatedTopics":["t17.3.8","t8.5.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why does taxation on Islamic banks' purchases create an uneven playing field?","options":["They pay less tax","Their sale/lease structures incur taxes and fees on asset purchases that conventional loans avoid","They are exempt","Their deposits are taxed twice as interest"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 470."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch18-t6"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch18-t6","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"18.3.2-B","sectionTitle":"Issues in Islamic Finance: Structure of Financial Institutions; Regulatory and Tax Issues","title":"Structural Identity of Islamic Banks and Their Regulatory/Tax Environment","covers":["t18.3.2","t18.3.2.b","t18.3.2.c"],"overview":"Islamic banks must decide their structural identity (trader vs. intermediary) while navigating a regulatory and tax environment often not designed for their asset-based operations, requiring specific reforms across ten areas.","simpleExplanation":"Should Islamic banks look and operate like conventional banks, or like trading companies? The author says they're genuinely different - they deal in goods, not money - and shouldn't treat their current short-term-commercial-lending-focused business model as untouchable; they need to expand into infrastructure/project financing and corporate advisory work too. Meanwhile, because tax and banking laws were mostly written with conventional banks in mind, Islamic banks often get unfairly taxed twice on the same transaction (since they have to actually buy and sell the asset), and regulators need to fix this and about nine other specific problems for Islamic banking to compete fairly.","academicExplanation":"On Structure: what should Islamic banks' structure be - traders or business entities? In most countries they operate analogously to conventional banks within national/international banking systems, functioning as financial intermediaries linking savers to economic/social development financing - a role all Islamic scholars accept - but their PROCEDURE differs: undertaking real sector business rather than dealing in money on interest, purchasing goods/assets on client requisition (hence AAOIFI's 'Murabaha to Purchase Orderer' Standard) rather than holding inventory. Concern exists that IFIs concentrate too much on short-term commercial financing like conventional banks, when an active developmental role is the actual rationale for their existence - a genuine concern requiring structural changes and legal/regulatory amendments to fix. Banking should not be treated as a 'sacred cow' precluding change in tools/processes/operations; survival in a rapidly transforming financial world requires adjustment. IFIs must diversify to offer broader portfolio services (savers and fund users alike) - increasingly providing project/infrastructure financing via Shirkah/Ijarah-based modes, corporate advisory services (Sukuk issuance, balance sheet/corporate restructuring via syndication), and the full spectrum from retail banking to fund management and corporate services, requiring close central bank/SEC coordination and possibly subsidiaries for specific sectors/operations engaging in genuine trade/leasing. On Regulatory and Tax Issues: like conventional institutions, IFIs need regulation for: (1) investor information availability; (2) saver interest protection; (3) Shari'ah compliance and financial system soundness; (4) a legal framework conducive to smooth functioning where 'cost of funds' cannot be recovered on default; (5) effective monetary policy/management. Since IFIs' operations differ fundamentally, they face different legal/regulatory/taxation problems - fostering stability requires uniform regulatory/transparency standards tailored to Islamic products/institutions' specific characteristics, incorporating international standards/core principles/good practices adapted to Islamic finance's needs. A specific problem: since Islamic banks must purchase assets for onward sale/lease, taxation/fees on those purchases create an uneven playing field versus conventional counterparts - leading banks in most (non-tax-free) jurisdictions to adopt practices raising Shari'ah-compliance doubts against AAOIFI/OIC Fiqh Council standards. Regulators in dual-system countries should recognize the need for flexible regulatory/tax frameworks (the UK FSA's flexibilities being a welcome model, hoped to continue toward making London an international Islamic finance hub). Ten specific steps needed for a comprehensive framework: (1) saving IFIs/customers from dual taxation (especially mortgage financing, Murabaha and leasing operations); (2) facilitating Shari'ah-related requirements fulfillment on both deposits and assets sides; (3) providing an effective Shari'ah compliance framework; (4) ensuring justifiable procedures for profit distribution between shareholders and depositor categories (more relevant here than in conventional finance); (5) increasing investor information to reduce adverse selection/moral hazard; (6) enforcing prudential rules/regulations suited to the new system's risk management needs; (7) ensuring current-account risks (an IFI liability) are borne by banks themselves, not transmitted to investment accounts, especially during distress; (8) vigorous central bank staff training for effective IFI supervision; (9) establishing a dedicated research/training centre for banking regulation/supervision/education; (10) providing specially-trained rating and feasibility-study institutions as system infrastructure. The author also critiques the broader regulatory environment: depositor-right protection is nominally the foremost regulatory objective globally, but in practice, 'liberalized' financial sectors see regulators/central banks NOT intervening in rate structures, when more intensive supervision is actually needed to protect depositors/entrepreneurs from exploitation by 'self-interest'/profit-maximizing institutions - free market policy has become a source of injustice for both conventional and Islamic bank clients, with bank spreads increasing rather than fair income shares passing to depositors, requiring proper regulatory vigilance especially for IFIs given their depositors' additional risk-bearing. On default specifically, regulators should introduce parameters distinguishing solvent/wilful defaulters (charged heavily as a deterrent) from those in genuine difficulty (given respite without charge), plus a well-thought-out liability-restructuring system for insolvent customers to aid business revival. Regulators should also enlist Shari'ah advisors with appropriate qualifications/skill sets, potentially helping train scholars in finance-oriented knowledge, and provide transparent charity-fund-disposal regulations (potentially requiring shareholder-income contributions too), while ensuring IFIs avoid lavish unnecessary marketing spend in favour of community/social development-based marketing.","examExplanation":"Structural identity: Islamic banks ARE financial intermediaries (accepted role) but with a different PROCEDURE (real goods, not money) - AAOIFI's 'Murabaha to Purchase Orderer' Standard reflects this. Concern: too much short-term commercial focus vs. needed developmental role - requires diversification into project/infrastructure financing and corporate advisory. Ten specific regulatory/tax reform areas needed (memorize the theme categories): dual taxation relief, Shari'ah facilitation, compliance framework, fair profit distribution, investor information, prudential rules, current-account risk isolation, staff training, research/training centre, rating/feasibility infrastructure.","keyPoints":["Islamic banks are accepted as genuine financial intermediaries by all scholars, but their operational procedure differs fundamentally - dealing in goods, not money, without holding full inventory (per AAOIFI's 'Murabaha to Purchase Orderer' Standard)","A genuine concern is that IFIs over-concentrate on short-term commercial financing when an active developmental role is their actual rationale for existence","Because Islamic banks must purchase assets for onward sale/lease, taxation on those purchases creates an uneven playing field versus conventional banks, sometimes leading to Shari'ah-compliance-questionable workarounds","The UK FSA's regulatory flexibility is cited as a welcome model, hoped to help make London an international Islamic finance hub","Ten specific regulatory/tax reforms are recommended, spanning dual taxation relief, Shari'ah compliance facilitation, fair profit distribution, investor information, prudential rules, current-account risk isolation, staff training, and dedicated research/rating infrastructure","Regulators should distinguish solvent/wilful defaulters (charged heavily as deterrent) from genuinely struggling debtors (given respite), with a liability-restructuring system for insolvent customers"],"definitions":[],"conditions":["Current-account-related risks (an IFI liability) must be borne by the bank itself, not transmitted to investment account holders, particularly when the bank is in distress"],"principles":["Banking should not be treated as a 'sacred cow' precluding necessary change in tools, processes or operations"],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Assuming Islamic banks should structurally resemble either a pure conventional bank or a pure trading company - the author argues they are financial intermediaries with a fundamentally different (goods-based, not money-based) operating procedure, a distinct third category"],"distinctions":["Islamic banks' accepted ROLE as financial intermediaries (same as conventional banks) versus their different PROCEDURE (dealing in real goods/assets, not money on interest)"],"relatedConcepts":["AAOIFI","Murabaha to Purchase Orderer","FSA (UK)","Dual taxation","Prudential regulation"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[469,470,471]}}]},{"id":"t18.3.2.c","section":"18.3.2","title":"Issues: Shari’ah Compliance Framework (Box 18.1)","pages":[472,474],"tier":"core","concepts":["shariah-governance","shariah-audit"],"intuition":"Who should supervise Shari’ah compliance — each bank, private boards, or a central board?","simpleExplanation":"Options: (1) a Shari’ah board in every IFI (costly, scarce scholars) or one advisor per IFI plus a national forum of advisors; (2) accredited private-sector Shari’ah boards/committees (but weak ongoing monitoring); (3) a central Shari’ah board advising regulators and coordinating with bank advisors — the author's preferred option, especially with a forum of advisors. Boards should supervise, not only approve, and inspect at least annually; windows should become stand-alone Islamic branches. Majority-based Fatwas (common in Sukuk endorsements) may harm integrity; AAOIFI standards should guide decisions. Inspection options: central bank, specialised Shari’ah audit firms, external CA firms (order depends on jurisdiction). Box 18.1: SBP's framework.","academicExplanation":["Box 18.1 (SBP): Shari’ah board at SBP since December 2003 — two scholars and three experts (banking, accounting, legal); each IBI must appoint a Shari’ah board or advisor under fit-and-proper criteria; internal Shari’ah audit at least annually; SBP inspection using a Shari’ah audit manual developed with a consultancy firm (first audit outsourced for capacity building).","Differences between a bank's Shari’ah department and auditors should be resolved via a jurisdiction-wide Shari’ah manual.","Penalties for lapses: non-compliant revenue to charity, borne by shareholders, not depositors; deposit-side irregularities need regulatory penalties."],"examExplanation":"Three framework options; author prefers central Shari’ah board + advisors' forum; boards must supervise; annual inspection; windows → branches; Box 18.1 SBP (2 scholars + 3 experts, 2003; advisor fit & proper; annual internal audit; SBP inspection manual); charity loss borne by shareholders.","keyPoints":["Frequent majority Fatwas may harm integrity.","Inspection options (Chapra and Khan): the central bank itself, private Shari’ah audit firms, or external CA firms. The best choice depends on the jurisdiction’s expertise (p. 473).","Differences between a bank’s Shari’ah department and its auditors are best settled through a jointly prepared Shari’ah manual (p. 474).","Windows should be replaced by stand-alone Islamic branches under Shari’ah monitoring (p. 473)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"SBP’s framework in brief","kind":"textbook","text":"A central-bank Shari’ah board (two scholars, three experts), a Shari’ah board or advisor in each bank under fit-and-proper criteria, internal Shari’ah audit at least yearly, and SBP inspection using a Shari’ah audit manual (pp. 473–474)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A Shari’ah board’s job ends when it approves a product.","right":"Boards should supervise application, finalise model agreements and inspect at least yearly. A passive role opens the door to interest in the garb of asset-based deals (p. 473)."},{"wrong":"Majority decisions are the normal basis for Shari’ah edicts.","right":"Most edicts are unanimous. Frequent resort to majority, as in some outsourced Sukuk endorsements, may harm integrity. AAOIFI Standards should be the basis (p. 472)."}],"relatedTopics":["t14.appx","t8.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which Shari’ah compliance option does the author consider best?","options":["No boards at all","A central Shari’ah board in the jurisdiction, with a forum of bank Shari’ah advisors","Only external CA firms","Only majority Fatwas"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 472."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch18-t7"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch18-t7","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"18.3.2-C","sectionTitle":"Issues in Islamic Finance: Shari'ah Compliance Framework","title":"Designing a Shari'ah Compliance Framework: Options and the Pakistan Model","covers":["t18.3.2","t18.3.2.b","t18.3.2.c"],"overview":"The author weighs several institutional options for organizing Shari'ah compliance across an industry, favouring a central Shari'ah board model complemented by individual bank advisors, illustrated by the State Bank of Pakistan's actual framework.","simpleExplanation":"Who should be in charge of checking that Islamic banks are actually following Shari'ah rules? The author walks through a few options - a full scholar board in every single bank (too expensive and there aren't enough qualified scholars), private-sector boards with no central bank involvement (weak oversight), or a single central Shari'ah board for the whole country working alongside individual bank advisors (the author's preferred option) - and uses Pakistan's actual central bank framework as a real working example of how this can be organized in practice, including how often audits should happen and who should conduct them.","academicExplanation":"While Shari'ah compliance's necessity is universally accepted, the appropriate FRAMEWORK for different situations remains an open issue. Option 1: a Shari'ah board (not just an advisor) in every individual IFI, to guide product development/application and enforce internal Shari'ah controls at the micro-level - problematic due to the shortage of competent Shari'ah scholars and sheer cost; a variation is one Shari'ah ADVISOR (not a full board) per IFI, combined with a central-bank- or IFI-association-facilitated forum where advisors periodically meet to discuss/resolve Shari'ah issues (economizing costs while enabling wider discussion, potentially standardizing edicts). Option 2: central banks/monetary authorities facilitate INDEPENDENT private-sector Shari'ah boards/committees (with both Shari'ah and banking expertise) providing advisory/consultancy services and periodic inspection across IFIs, requiring central bank accreditation (fit-and-proper, good governance criteria) for integrity/competence - the downside being that effective operational monitoring and time-sensitive Shari'ah guidance for bankers becomes harder without direct central-bank/individual-IFI Shari'ah presence. Option 3 (the author's preferred option): a CENTRAL Shari'ah board at the country/jurisdiction level, advising regulators on Shari'ah issues and coordinating with individual banks' Shari'ah advisors/boards to ensure compliance - potentially bringing harmony to IFI practices across a jurisdiction, made even more useful if combined with the proposed forum of Shari'ah advisors. A related, unresolved question: should Shari'ah board membership be pure scholars, or include other-discipline experts (banking, accountancy, law, economics)? Most boards currently comprise Shari'ah scholars with banking/finance understanding, co-opting other-discipline experts only as needed for technical help; edicts are mostly unanimous, occasionally majority-based (especially when Shari'ah endorsement is outsourced) - the author notes the majority principle could be acceptable with sufficient accepted-Shari'ah-principle grounds, but frequent/open resort to it may harm board/system integrity long-term; one proposed solution is to make AAOIFI Standards the settled basis for board decisions/edicts, applied meticulously, to avoid differences. Whichever option is chosen, ensuring compliance requires more input from BOTH banks and regulators: full-fledged Shari'ah departments in all IFIs, effective internal Shari'ah controls, and Shari'ah inspection of operations - with banks operating 'Islamic windows' potentially required to establish stand-alone Islamic banking branches instead, under Shari'ah monitoring guidance. Shari'ah boards/advisors should SUPERVISE (not just advise) IFI activities to ensure full compliance, finalizing model agreements/application procedures and verifying banks follow them in letter and spirit - a merely passive, approval-only role (leaving application entirely to the banks) opens the door to interest disguised as asset-based transactions; experts deem it necessary for Shari'ah boards to thoroughly inspect IFI activities at least ANNUALLY. Three suggested Shari'ah-inspection options, in ascending order of preference: (1) inspection by central banks themselves; (2) inspection by specially-created private-sector Shari'ah audit firms; (3) inspection by external CA/audit firms - though this ordering may vary by jurisdiction depending on the central bank inspection team's competence/training/equipment. Box 18.1 details the State Bank of Pakistan's (SBP) actual Shari'ah compliance framework: a Shari'ah board (two Shari'ah scholars, three experts in banking/accounting/legal framework) established at the central bank in December 2003, advising on modes/procedures/laws/regulation; each Islamic banking institution (IBI) must appoint at least a Shari'ah advisor per SBP-board-approved fit-and-proper criteria; each IBI conducts internal Shari'ah audit at least annually; SBP provides Shari'ah compliance audit via its own inspection staff, using a manual prepared with a reputable consultancy firm (the first Shari'ah audit was outsourced to that firm for SBP staff capacity-building, with the manual finalized based on lessons from that inspection). A remaining problem: resolving differences of opinion between a bank's own Shari'ah department and the Shari'ah auditors - best solved by jointly preparing (auditors/regulators, various Shari'ah boards, practitioners) a Shari'ah manual for the jurisdiction, with audits conducted on that manual's basis. On penalizing IFIs for Shari'ah lapses: on the assets side, non-compliant transaction revenue goes to the Charity Account, but this LOSS should belong to shareholders (not depositors), since depositors furnish funds expecting Shari'ah-compliant business and should not be penalized for management's failures; for deposits-side irregularities, regulators must enforce a penalty set in consultation with auditors and Shari'ah scholars.","examExplanation":"Three institutional options for Shari'ah compliance framework: (1) full board per IFI (costly, scholar shortage); (2) private-sector boards outside central bank (weaker operational oversight); (3) central jurisdiction-level board + individual bank advisors/forum (author's preferred option). Shari'ah boards should SUPERVISE (not just approve) - annual inspection minimum. Three inspection-provider options, ascending preference: central bank staff, private Shari'ah audit firms, external CA/audit firms. Pakistan (SBP) model: central Shari'ah board (2 scholars + 3 experts) since Dec 2003; each IBI needs at least an advisor; annual internal audit mandatory; SBP conducts its own compliance audit via trained inspection staff. Penalty allocation: non-compliant income loss belongs to SHAREHOLDERS, not depositors.","keyPoints":["The author's preferred Shari'ah compliance framework option is a central, jurisdiction-level Shari'ah board coordinating with individual banks' Shari'ah advisors/boards, ideally combined with a forum of Shari'ah advisors","A purely private-sector board structure (with no central bank/individual-IFI Shari'ah presence) makes time-sensitive operational guidance and monitoring harder","Shari'ah boards/advisors should SUPERVISE (not merely approve) IFI activities; a passive, approval-only role risks interest disguised as asset-based transactions","Experts recommend thorough Shari'ah inspection of IFI activities at least annually, with central bank inspection generally preferred over private audit firms where the central bank team is competent","The State Bank of Pakistan established a central Shari'ah board (two scholars, three banking/accounting/legal experts) in December 2003, requiring each Islamic banking institution to have at least a Shari'ah advisor and conduct annual internal Shari'ah audit","Revenue lost to non-Shari'ah-compliant transactions (directed to the Charity Account) should be borne by shareholders, not depositors, since depositors expect Shari'ah-compliant business and should not be penalized for management's failures"],"definitions":[{"term":"Islamic Banking Institution (IBI)","definition":"A term used by the State Bank of Pakistan's regulatory framework to refer to any bank or institution conducting Islamic banking business, each required to appoint at least a Shari'ah advisor meeting SBP-approved fit-and-proper criteria."}],"conditions":["Each Islamic Banking Institution must appoint at least a Shari'ah advisor meeting fit-and-proper criteria approved by the central Shari'ah board","Each Islamic Banking Institution must conduct an internal Shari'ah audit at least once a year","Non-Shari'ah-compliant transaction revenue losses should be borne by shareholders, not by depositors"],"principles":["A Shari'ah board's role should be supervisory, not merely advisory/approval-only, to avoid interest disguised as asset-based transactions"],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"State Bank of Pakistan's Shari'ah Compliance Framework (Box 18.1)","kind":"textbook","text":"SBP established a central Shari'ah board (two Shari'ah scholars, three experts in banking, accounting and legal framework) in December 2003 to advise on modes, procedures, laws and regulation; each Islamic Banking Institution must appoint at least a Shari'ah advisor per SBP-approved criteria and conduct annual internal Shari'ah audit, while SBP itself conducts periodic Shari'ah compliance inspection via trained staff using a dedicated manual, whose first application was outsourced to a consultancy firm for capacity-building purposes."}],"commonConfusions":["Assuming a full Shari'ah scholar board in every individual bank is the ideal, cost-free solution - the author identifies scholar scarcity and cost as real practical barriers to this option"],"distinctions":["A Shari'ah board's supervisory role (finalizing model agreements, verifying actual application, at least annual inspection) versus a merely advisory/approval role (approving products/procedures but leaving application entirely to the bank)"],"relatedConcepts":["Shari'ah board","Shari'ah advisor","State Bank of Pakistan","Charity Account","AAOIFI"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[471,472,473,474]}}],"table":{"caption":"Shari’ah compliance framework options (pp. 472–473)","head":["Option","Strength","Weakness"],"rows":[["Board in every IFI","Close guidance and internal controls","Shortage of scholars; cost"],["One advisor per IFI plus a national forum","Economical; wider debate; standard edicts","Less depth per bank"],["Accredited private-sector Shari’ah firms","Independent expertise","Weak ongoing monitoring and advice"],["Central Shari’ah board plus bank advisors (author’s preference)","Harmony across the jurisdiction","Needs coordination; best with a forum"]]}},{"id":"t18.3.3","section":"18.3.3","title":"The Challenges","pages":[474,479],"tier":"core","concepts":["awareness","benchmark","liquidity","human-resources"],"intuition":"What seven challenges will decide the future of Islamic finance?","simpleExplanation":"(1) Education and awareness — the greatest challenge: explain that practice evolves while philosophy is unchanged, that Islamic banking is not free lending, and educate religious leaders. (2) Shari’ah compliance and integrity — grey-area products (Tawarruq, buy-back) threaten the movement; Siddiqi urges redefining ‘Shari’ah-compliant’ to include Maslaha and Maqasid; Moore's four questions. (3) Competitiveness in a dual system — regulations treating deposits as capital-guaranteed conflict with PLS; mutual-fund structures may help. (4) Developing benchmarks (e.g. Mirakhor and ul Haque, 1998). (5) Product development and public finance. (6) Liquidity and monetary management — SLR without return; interbank Mudarabah markets; lender-of-last-resort options. (7) Human resources — training, R&D and university involvement.","academicExplanation":["Moore's four questions: Does it comply with Shari’ah? Is it the best investment for the client? Does it create value for the client and society? Would the banker invest his own money? The author agrees compliance must be accompanied by the best solutions for clients.","Lender of last resort options: (1) short Mudarabah facility (e.g. 2–3 days, 50:50 on daily product, ratio rising in the central bank's favour if not repaid on time); (2) a common pool with the central bank funded by IFIs, offering interest-free accommodation for up to three days in a defined crunch.","Securitised or fund certificates should have cash and receivables below 50% to enjoy a genuine secondary market.","Conventional banks' Islamic business may receive IFIs' funds if segregation is certified."],"examExplanation":"Seven challenges: awareness (greatest), compliance/integrity (Siddiqi's Maqasid; Moore's 4 questions), competitiveness, benchmarks, product development, liquidity/monetary management (LOLR options), HR.","keyPoints":["Last revealed verse (2:281) on accountability cited for compliance.","Any unacceptable minority interpretation damages the industry's image.","Awareness is the greatest challenge: people must learn that the philosophy has not changed while practice evolves, and that Islamic banking is not free lending (pp. 474–475).","Siddiqi urges redefining “Shari’ah-compliant” to include Maslaha and Maqasid, with economists involved (p. 475).","To compete, IFIs need compliant products, best-practice screening and risk management, innovation and better service (p. 477).","Securitised funds should hold less than 50 % in cash and receivables to support a genuine secondary market (p. 478).","HR needs a mindset of commitment as well as competence; universities should focus on evolved practice, not controversies (p. 479)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Mutual-fund structure for deposits","kind":"textbook","text":"Where banking law requires capital-guaranteed deposits but the Shari’ah requires PLS, operating as mutual funds could resolve the conflict and pass on more real-sector profit (p. 477)."},{"title":"Mirakhor and ul Haque (1998)","kind":"textbook","text":"This was an early effort to develop indices for rates of return on national participation papers, as a step towards Islamic benchmarks (p. 477)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Using a conventional benchmark is prohibited for Islamic banks.","right":"It is permissible as a pricing tool, but a benchmark reflecting fictitious assets will not serve Islamic finance’s socio-economic aims. Real benchmarks are a long-term goal (p. 477)."},{"wrong":"Islamic banks are advantaged on statutory liquidity reserves.","right":"They must hold a larger share of deposits with the central bank without return, which hurts their competitiveness (p. 478)."}],"relatedTopics":["t17.4.3","t14.4.4","t16.7"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which challenge does the author call the greatest?","options":["Benchmarks","Creating understanding and awareness of Islamic finance","Taxation","Cards"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 475."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch18-t8","ch18-t9","ch18-t10"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch18-t8","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"18.3.3-A","sectionTitle":"The Challenges: Education and Awareness; Shari'ah Compliance; Competitiveness","title":"Education, Shari'ah Compliance Integrity and Competing with Conventional Banks","covers":["t18.3.3"],"overview":"The author's top three named challenges are creating public awareness/education, achieving genuine (not merely formal) Shari'ah compliance, and competing effectively within a dual conventional-Islamic financial system without compromising Shari'ah principles.","simpleExplanation":"For Islamic finance to keep growing, three big challenges need solving: people (both everyday savers and religious leaders) need real education about how it actually works, since myths and misunderstanding still hold many people back; the industry needs to genuinely live up to its Shari'ah promises rather than just formally checking boxes (one scholar suggests even redefining what 'Shari'ah-compliant' really means to include broader considerations); and Islamic banks need to compete effectively against conventional banks - offering good products and services - without watering down their principles just to keep up.","academicExplanation":"Education and Awareness Creation: future growth depends on enhancing the industry's clientele, achievable only through education, myth-removal, and awareness-building - economists, policymakers and the general public (Muslim-majority and minority countries alike) have many unanswered queries (does it work? is it sustainable competing with a centuries-old system? are the products really Islamic? can it reduce hardship?), which bankers must confidently address; savers who've avoided banking altogether due to Riba-avoidance will only approach Islamic banks once assured of genuine Shari'ah-compliant fund use. Critics note the concept's apparent change since the 1970s - people need to understand this reflects evolving PRACTICE from an unchanged underlying PHILOSOPHY (goods/services as the banking subject matter, transactions linked to real sector activity). Failure to create sufficient awareness could cause serious market disruptions and systemic risk for the nascent industry; a comprehensive campaign must clarify Islamic banking is NOT free loaning to business/industry, and that savers can justifiably earn returns based on transaction nature and business results - awareness-raising is especially needed among grass-roots religious leaders. Shari'ah Compliance and Industry Integrity: crucial for credibility, requiring Islamic bankers to ensure genuine Shari'ah conformity (grounded in universal accountability to Allah, per the Qur'an's last revealed verse, 2:281, placed right after the Riba verses). Practically, many IFIs - especially those operating 'windows' without effective internal Shari'ah controls - use Tawarruq, buy-back arrangements and other grey-area instruments so bluntly that, unchecked, they could 'betray the whole movement'. The author quotes M. Nejatullah Siddiqi's call for redefining 'Shari'ah-compliant' beyond mere analogical matching of new-to-old approved contracts, to explicitly incorporate Maslaha (public interest) and Maqasid al-Shari'ah (objectives of Shari'ah) - requiring strong involvement of trained economists/social scientists alongside traditionally-trained Shari'ah advisors, since traditional training alone cannot grasp the necessary economic analysis. Real-sector business involvement by IFIs is necessary to escape severe criticism and achieve genuine socio-economic impact; the common perception that Islamic banks don't actually carry out trading/leasing/construction and 'end up doing only financial operations' needs correcting. Shari'ah compliance requires both Shari'ah inspiration/full principle observance AND internal controls/inspection for credibility, but doesn't diminish the importance of other professional success requirements - the author cites Phillip Moore's (1997) four questions a Shari'ah board should typically ask about any transaction: (1) does it comply with Shari'ah law? (2) is it the best investment for the client? (3) does it produce value for the client and community/society? (4) would the banker personally invest their own money in it as an asset manager? If any answer is 'no', the transaction is usually rejected - though committees formally have power to reject only on Shari'ah non-compliance grounds; the author agrees Shari'ah compliance must be accompanied by genuinely the best solutions for clients' financial problems. Competitiveness and Parallel Functioning: the dominant current model is a DUAL system with interest-based and Islamic institutions operating side by side - Islamic finance's growth challenges conventional banks, while conventional banks' adoption of Islamic modes challenges Islamic banks, pointing to increasing future competition alongside a growing need for inter-system cooperation. Given IFIs' currently low market share, functioning competitively is genuinely difficult: they cannot offer depositor rates significantly different from conventional benchmarks (given regulatory requirements and competitive market forces), making it harder to attract corporate-sector deposits (where financial managers prioritize highest risk-free return) or compete on the financing side while ensuring Shari'ah compliance - requiring strategy/structure changes achieved via coordination with regulators, Shari'ah scholars and customers. Prospering competitively sustainably requires: conformity of products/transactions with Shari'ah principles; best-practice investment screening alongside credit/market/operational risk management; innovative products; and better customer service quality. Structural adjustments needed include dealing with real sector business and implementing both PLS and 'fixed-rate' Islamic modes; a specific legal-mismatch problem exists where banking laws in many jurisdictions require deposits to be capital-guaranteed while Shari'ah requires profit/loss sharing - potentially resolvable by IFIs operating in mutual-fund form, enabling higher real-sector-typical profits and passing a greater profit share to savers/investors.","examExplanation":"Three top-named challenges: (1) education/awareness (the greatest challenge per the text, especially at grass-roots religious-leader level); (2) Shari'ah compliance/integrity, including Siddiqi's call to redefine 'Shari'ah-compliant' to incorporate Maslaha and Maqasid al-Shari'ah, not just contract-matching analogy; (3) competitiveness in a dual conventional-Islamic system, requiring product conformity, risk screening, innovation and service quality - with mutual-fund structuring as one proposed fix for the deposit-guarantee-vs-PLS legal mismatch. Phillip Moore's four Shari'ah board questions: compliance, best investment, value to client/community, banker's own-money test.","keyPoints":["The author frames creating public education and awareness (removing myths, especially among grass-roots religious leaders) as the most necessary challenge for future growth","M. Nejatullah Siddiqi calls for redefining 'Shari'ah-compliant' to incorporate Maslaha (public interest) and Maqasid al-Shari'ah, not just analogical matching to approved contracts - requiring trained economists/social scientists alongside traditional Shari'ah scholars","Phillip Moore's four questions a Shari'ah board should ask about any transaction: Shari'ah compliance, best investment for the client, value to client/community, and whether the banker would personally invest their own money in it","The dominant current model is a dual system (Islamic and conventional institutions operating side by side), creating both increasing competition and growing need for cooperation","IFIs cannot offer depositor rates significantly different from conventional benchmarks given regulatory requirements and competitive forces, making corporate-sector deposit competition genuinely difficult","A specific legal mismatch (banking laws requiring capital-guaranteed deposits vs. Shari'ah requiring profit/loss sharing) could potentially be resolved by IFIs operating in mutual-fund form"],"definitions":[{"term":"Maslaha","definition":"Public interest or welfare consideration, which M. Nejatullah Siddiqi argues should be an essential part of a redefined, comprehensive understanding of 'Shari'ah-compliant', alongside Maqasid al-Shari'ah."}],"conditions":[],"principles":["Shari'ah compliance must be accompanied by genuinely the best solutions for clients' financial problems, not compliance alone"],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Phillip Moore's Four Shari'ah Board Questions","kind":"textbook","text":"Per Phillip Moore's 'Islamic Finance: A Partnership for Growth' (1997), a Shari'ah board will typically ask: (1) do the terms comply with Shari'ah law? (2) is this the best investment for the client? (3) does the investment produce value for the client and the community/society? (4) would the banker, as an asset manager, be prepared to invest their own money in this transaction? A 'no' to any usually leads to rejection, though the committee's formal power to reject rests only on Shari'ah non-compliance grounds."}],"commonConfusions":["Assuming 'Shari'ah-compliant' is a narrow, purely technical legal-matching exercise - Siddiqi's proposed redefinition explicitly broadens it to include Maslaha and Maqasid al-Shari'ah, requiring economic and social-science expertise, not just traditional jurisprudence"],"distinctions":["A Shari'ah board's formal power (reject only for Shari'ah non-compliance) versus Phillip Moore's broader four-question standard (which also considers investment quality, client/community value, and the banker's own confidence in the deal)"],"relatedConcepts":["Maqasid al-Shari'ah","M. Nejatullah Siddiqi","Islamic window","Mutual fund structuring"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[474,475,476,477]}},{"id":"ch18-t9","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"18.3.3-B","sectionTitle":"The Challenges: Developing Benchmarks; Product Development; Liquidity and Monetary Management; Human Resources","title":"Benchmarks, Product Innovation, Liquidity Management and Human Resource Development","covers":["t18.3.3"],"overview":"The remaining major challenges are developing independent Islamic pricing benchmarks, innovating new products (especially for public finance), building Islamic liquidity/monetary management tools including a central-bank lender-of-last-resort mechanism, and closing the severe shortage of trained Islamic finance professionals.","simpleExplanation":"Four more big challenges remain: figuring out fair local pricing benchmarks (instead of just using conventional interest rates everywhere), inventing genuinely new products especially for government financing needs, building Islamic-friendly tools for banks and central banks to manage cash shortages and surpluses (since Islamic banks currently get zero return on the money they're required to park with the central bank), and training enough qualified people who understand both finance and Islamic principles, since right now there simply aren't enough of them.","academicExplanation":"Developing Benchmarks: IFIs need benchmarks for pricing goods/services and determining joint-venture profit-sharing ratios; such benchmarks differ across jurisdictions/sectors/subsectors and require deep study accounting for development level, supply/demand, and customer assets/liabilities - needed at both international and country levels (citing Abbas Mirakhor and Nadeem ul Haque's 1998 effort developing national participation paper (NPP) return-calculation indices as one such attempt). For now, conventional benchmarks are used almost everywhere; while permissible as a pricing tool for goods/usufruct, a benchmark reflecting fictitious assets (as in the conventional framework) will not help realize Islamic banking's socio-economic objectives - requiring long-term, sustained effort from economists, bankers, policymakers and Shari'ah scholars. Product Development - Financial Engineering: innovative products for cash management and financing various sectors (especially government/public sector) cannot be overemphasized, requiring mutual effort from economists, practitioners, Shari'ah scholars and regulators; the major challenge is ensuring Shari'ah compliance consistent with mainstream developed theory - any Shari'ah interpretation unacceptable to the scholarly majority would damage the industry's long-term image. Public finance product development, in collaboration with fiscal authorities, has much work remaining; Sukuk/securitization potential can help significantly. In genuine need cases, Shari'ah boards may offer well-defined, specified relaxations without compromising cardinal Shari'ah principles - but vague observations/edicts risk anarchy and loss of integrity for both the theory and the scholars themselves. Liquidity and Monetary Management: this area has seen insufficient work so far; a related issue is the availability of liquidity management instruments. Compared to conventional institutions, IFIs must place a GREATER portion of deposits with central banks as statutory liquidity reserves (SLR), earning NO RETURN on them - harming their competitiveness in offering depositors competitive returns. While some liquidity management instruments have developed, the modus operandi for using them in open market operations (OMOs) and repo transactions still needs to evolve. Establishing inter-bank Islamic money markets in jurisdictions with significant IFI presence is key for short-term liquidity/monetary management and Islamic finance promotion generally; demand-supply mismatches are normally covered via inter-bank transactions on the Mudarabah principle. Islamic banks can also invest with conventional banks offering Islamic banking business, provided the latter invests on a Shari'ah-compliant basis with Shari'ah-advisor-certified segregation of Riba-free and Riba-based deposits/financing. Banks may also securitize assets or manage funds via certificates of deposit/investment tradable in the secondary market (requiring cash/receivables components below 50% of the securitized pool/fund assets, ensuring a genuine secondary market for genuinely asset-based certificates). Regarding the central bank as lender of last resort (the conventional system's most strategic feature), the author proposes two Islamic-framework options: (1) a Mudarabah-based facility for a defined-extent liquidity crisis (e.g. 2-3 days), with a profit-sharing ratio (e.g. 50:50) on a daily-product basis, potentially shifting more favourably toward the central bank if the IFI cannot repay by the due date; (2) establishing a common pool at the central bank, funded by all IFIs contributing a specified deposit percentage, giving members the right to interest-free accommodation for up to (three) days during a defined liquidity crunch. Supply of Well-versed Human Resources: a major current bottleneck is the scarcity of trained human resources; though IFIs are intermediaries like conventional banks, Islamic bankers need a significantly different mindset and special orientation - all incumbents should be technically competent AND well aware of/committed to Islamic finance principles, since only committed, ethically-embedded, competent personnel can market products effectively and enhance the discipline's acceptability. Long-term growth requires an HR development framework, boosted R&D activity, and enhanced training/education facilities in collaboration with leading academic/research institutions - requiring joint effort from IFIs, Shari'ah scholars, central banks/regulators, universities, business schools and the student community, with universities recognizing the huge need for competent human resources, focusing on the philosophy AND practical operations of Islamic finance as it has evolved, avoiding unnecessary controversy.","examExplanation":"Benchmark development: cited effort = Mirakhor and ul Haque's 1998 National Participation Paper (NPP) return-index work. Product development challenge: Shari'ah interpretation unacceptable to the majority damages long-term industry image - relaxations must be well-defined, not vague. Liquidity management key facts: IFIs get NO RETURN on statutory liquidity reserves (SLR) placed with central banks (competitiveness disadvantage); inter-bank Islamic money markets use Mudarabah principle; secondary-market certificates require cash/receivables below 50% of pool. Central bank lender-of-last-resort options: (1) Mudarabah-based facility (e.g. 2-3 days, 50:50 ratio, shifts toward central bank on late repayment); (2) common pool at central bank funded by IFI contributions, giving interest-free accommodation rights (e.g. up to 3 days). HR: scarcity is a major bottleneck, requiring joint university-industry-regulator collaboration.","keyPoints":["IFIs currently place a greater portion of deposits with central banks as statutory liquidity reserves (SLR) than conventional banks, earning no return on them - a competitiveness disadvantage","Inter-bank Islamic money market transactions covering demand-supply mismatches are typically conducted on the Mudarabah principle","Certificates of deposit/investment from securitized bank assets/funds require the cash/receivables component to stay below 50% of the pool/fund's assets, to maintain a genuine secondary market","Two proposed Islamic central-bank lender-of-last-resort options: a short-term Mudarabah-based facility (e.g. 2-3 days, profit-sharing ratio like 50:50, shifting toward the central bank if unpaid on time), or a common pool funded by all IFIs offering interest-free short-term accommodation","A conventional benchmark reflecting fictitious assets will not help realize Islamic banking's socio-economic objectives, making the development of genuinely Islamic benchmarks a long-term, multi-stakeholder priority","The scarcity of trained human resources - technically competent AND committed to Islamic finance principles - is identified as a major current bottleneck for the industry's growth"],"definitions":[{"term":"Statutory Liquidity Requirement (SLR)","definition":"A portion of deposits that banks (including Islamic banks) must place with the central bank as a reserve requirement; IFIs earn no return on this placement, creating a competitiveness disadvantage relative to conventional banks."},{"term":"National Participation Paper (NPP)","definition":"A concept explored by Abbas Mirakhor and Nadeem ul Haque (1998) involving the development of indices for calculating rates of return, as an effort toward genuinely Islamic pricing benchmarks."}],"conditions":["The cash and receivables component in a securitized pool of assets or fund should be less than 50% of total assets to maintain genuine secondary-market tradability"],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Proposed Mudarabah-Based Lender-of-Last-Resort Facility","kind":"textbook","text":"For a defined-extent liquidity crisis, a Mudarabah-based facility could be provided for two or three days with a profit-sharing ratio (e.g. 50:50) calculated on a daily-product basis; the agreement could stipulate that if the IFI is unable to repay by the due date, the profit-sharing ratio shifts more favourably toward the central bank."}],"commonConfusions":["Assuming Islamic banks earn the same competitive advantage as conventional banks on their central bank reserve placements - IFIs typically earn no return on their (often larger) statutory liquidity reserves, a real competitiveness disadvantage the chapter highlights"],"distinctions":["A Mudarabah-based central bank liquidity facility (profit-sharing, ratio potentially shifting on late repayment) versus a common-pool interest-free accommodation facility (fixed short-term window, funded by IFI contributions)"],"relatedConcepts":["Mudarabah","Statutory Liquidity Requirement","Open market operations","Securitization"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[477,478,479]}},{"id":"ch18-t10","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"18.4","sectionTitle":"Conclusion","title":"Chapter and Book Conclusion","covers":["t18.3.3"],"overview":"The book closes by affirming Islamic banking's bright but challenging prospects, requiring structural adjustment, mindset change, coordinated multi-stakeholder collaboration, and genuine real-sector engagement to realize its full socio-economic potential.","simpleExplanation":"The book ends on a hopeful but realistic note: Islamic finance has real, sustainable, profitable potential as a genuine business - not just a religious formality - but getting there requires real changes in how business is done and how people think, strong government/regulator support, and genuine involvement in real economic activity rather than just avoiding the word 'interest' on paper. The goal isn't just following the letter of the rules, but delivering real positive impact on society through fair pricing, cost efficiency and true economic development.","academicExplanation":"The author affirms Islamic banking/finance's prospects are bright, though the task ahead is challenging: its practice is sustainable AND profitable as a genuine business, but requires changes in business procedures/tools and stakeholder mindset, plus coordinated work to develop innovative products within Shari'ah boundaries. This requires promoting collaboration across levels - global and local, public and private sectors, business/industry and academia, Shari'ah scholars and practitioners - with crucial government/regulator support, justified on both principled and practical grounds. Realizing Islamic finance's potential requires structural adjustments enabling IFIs to handle real sector business, implementing trading/leasing/real-estate contracts using Islamic modes; the author reiterates that fixity of rates is NOT a problem at all - nonfixity of prices/rentals would actually make transactions Shari'ah-invalid. A genuine trading/leasing mindset would let IFIs earn the higher profits real-sector businesses normally earn, passing a greater profit share to savers/investors; beyond securitizing asset portfolios, IFIs should pursue fund management, investing in both Shirkah-based variable-income and trade/leasing-based quasi-fixed-income operations to serve investors across risk profiles/preferences. Islamic banks' operations must have POSITIVE socio-economic implications through real sector development and just/equitable pricing policies, alongside cost efficiency and profit adequacy - IFIs must look beyond the FORMAL abolition of interest and practically engage in real business activities. The author quotes Zubair Hasan (economist, International Islamic University Malaysia): 'One must see Islamic banking as an on-going process in a social milieu characterized with mass poverty and gross inequalities in the wealth, income and opportunities' - framing the whole project as a continuing developmental process, not a finished product. Regulators, government authorities and practitioners must jointly address: education, training and public awareness; effective contract enforcement; strengthened recovery systems; and internal/external Shari'ah compliance audit of IFI operations. Strengthening the regulatory set-up, facilitating suitable IFI products, designing prudential rules reflecting Islamic contracts' specific risk characteristics, and applying well-thought-out risk management/accounting standards will help deepen and widen the financial sector - regulators must provide an enabling environment ensuring good corporate governance, transparency and Shari'ah compliance are genuinely in place. Finally, the author closes by noting the use of Islamic finance principles for PUBLIC SECTOR financing specifically needs more attention, since it could help ensure fiscal discipline and provide a just basis for monetary management at the state level - directly echoing the macro-level policy agenda the chapter opened with.","examExplanation":"Closing framing: Islamic banking is 'an ongoing process' (Zubair Hasan quote), not a finished product - operating in a context of mass poverty/inequality it aims to address. Key reiterated point: fixity of rates is NOT itself a problem (nonfixity would actually invalidate transactions); the real requirement is genuine real-sector engagement, going 'beyond the formal abolition of interest'. Final emphasis: public sector financing via Islamic finance principles needs more attention for fiscal discipline and just monetary management - closing the loop back to Chapter 18's opening macro-policy agenda.","keyPoints":["The author reiterates that fixity of rates/prices/rentals is NOT itself a Shari'ah problem - nonfixity would actually make many transactions invalid","IFIs must look beyond the formal (technical) abolition of interest and practically engage in real business activities to achieve genuine socio-economic impact","Zubair Hasan's quoted framing presents Islamic banking as 'an on-going process' operating within a context of mass poverty and gross inequality, not a finished or static system","The conclusion calls for coordinated collaboration across global/local, public/private, industry/academia, and Shari'ah-scholar/practitioner lines, with crucial government and regulator support","The final specific recommendation is that Islamic finance principles for PUBLIC SECTOR financing need more attention, to help ensure fiscal discipline and provide a just basis for monetary management","This closing recommendation directly echoes the chapter's opening macro-level policy agenda about disciplining state-level financial creation and management"],"definitions":[],"conditions":[],"principles":["Islamic banks' operations must have positive socio-economic implications through real sector development and just/equitable pricing policies, in addition to cost efficiency and profit adequacy"],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Zubair Hasan's Framing of Islamic Banking","kind":"textbook","text":"Zubair Hasan, an economist at the International Islamic University of Malaysia, wrote: 'One must see Islamic banking as an on-going process in a social milieu characterized with mass poverty and gross inequalities in the wealth, income and opportunities' - framing the whole Islamic finance project as continuing developmental work rather than a completed achievement."}],"commonConfusions":["Assuming the book's conclusion presents Islamic finance as a finished, fully successful system - the closing framing explicitly describes it as 'an on-going process' still working to realize its potential within a challenging social context"],"distinctions":["Formal (technical) abolition of interest versus genuine, practical engagement in real business activities - the author insists the latter is necessary, not just the former"],"relatedConcepts":["Real sector development","Shari'ah compliance audit","Public sector financing","Fiscal discipline"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.4","pages":[479,480]}}],"table":{"caption":"Challenges and suggested responses (Section 18.3.3)","head":["Challenge","Suggested response"],"rows":[["Education and awareness","Campaigns; educate religious leaders; explain evolution of practice"],["Compliance and integrity","Maqasid-based definition; real-sector involvement; Moore's four questions"],["Competitiveness","Compliant, innovative products; quality service; mutual-fund structures"],["Benchmarks","Research on real-economy benchmarks (Mirakhor & ul Haque)"],["Product development","Joint work of economists, bankers, scholars, regulators; public finance via Sukuk"],["Liquidity and monetary management","Interbank Mudarabah; securitisation (<50% cash/receivables); LOLR Mudarabah facility or common pool"],["Human resources","Training, R&D, universities and business schools"]]}}],"questions":[{"id":"q18.1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which is one of the international measures proposed in Section 18.2?","options":["A single global reserve currency","Increasing the number of reserve currencies, including regional ones","Abolishing all currencies","Expanding interest-based aid"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 458.","topicId":"t18.2","chapter":18,"section":"18.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall policy agenda","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2","pages":[457,459]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.2","type":"tf","prompt":"The author argues that Islamic finance limited to retail and corporate banking, while governments borrow on interest, will have a visible macro impact.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"p. 460.","topicId":"t18.2.1","chapter":18,"section":"18.2.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Understand macro argument","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2.1","pages":[459,461]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.3","type":"multi","prompt":"Which are listed as factors on which IFIs' integrity depends? (Select all.)","options":["Shari’ah compliance of products","Impact on clients and society","Professional competence","Size of branch network","Behaviour and observance of Shari’ah norms by staff"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2,4],"explanation":"p. 467.","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall integrity factors","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[465,469]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.4","type":"match","prompt":"Match the challenge with a suggested response.","pairs":[["Awareness","Educate public and religious leaders"],["Benchmarks","Research on real-economy rates"],["Liquidity","Interbank Mudarabah and LOLR facility"],["Human resources","Training with universities"]],"explanation":"Section 18.3.3.","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Match challenges","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[474,479]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.5","type":"identify","prompt":"A central bank Shari’ah board of two scholars and three experts established in December 2003 belongs to:","options":["Bank Negara Malaysia","State Bank of Pakistan","Central Bank of Bahrain","Bank of England"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Box 18.1.","topicId":"t18.3.2.c","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify Box 18.1","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[472,474]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.6","type":"scenario","prompt":"An Islamic bank pays depositors a fixed-looking return by secretly transferring shareholders' profit to match competitors. The author's view:","options":["Acceptable competitive practice","Against the spirit of Shari’ah unless done transparently (e.g. disclosed profit equalisation reserves)","Required by regulators","Irrelevant to integrity"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 468–469.","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Evaluate practice","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[465,469]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.7","type":"order","prompt":"Order the phased transformation requirements in Section 18.2.1.","items":["Identify legal, regulatory and institutional requirements","Lay down a phased plan and implementation mechanism","Feedback and continuous review"],"explanation":"p. 460.","topicId":"t18.2.1","chapter":18,"section":"18.2.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Sequence transformation","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2.1","pages":[459,461]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.8","type":"comparison","prompt":"Which statement correctly compares the Shari’ah framework options?","options":["Private boards provide the best continuous monitoring","A board in every IFI is costly given scarce scholars; a central board with an advisors' forum harmonises practice","No option needs regulators","External CA firms are always best"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 472–473.","topicId":"t18.3.2.c","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Compare frameworks","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[472,474]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.9","type":"short","prompt":"Explain two Islamic alternatives to the conventional lender-of-last-resort facility proposed by the author.","keywords":["Mudarabah","daily product","common pool","interest-free","three days"],"correctAnswer":"(1) A Mudarabah-based facility for a defined liquidity crisis, e.g. two or three days with a 50:50 profit-sharing ratio on daily product, with the ratio rising in the central bank's favour if the IFI fails to repay on time. (2) A common pool at the central bank funded by all IFIs at a specified percentage of deposits, from which they may receive interest-free accommodation for up to three days during a defined liquidity crunch.","explanation":"p. 478.","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain LOLR options","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[474,479]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.10","type":"tf","prompt":"According to the author, product innovation requires an Islamic alternative for every conventional product.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"p. 466.","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Understand innovation limits","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[465,469]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.11","type":"definition","prompt":"In Moore's four questions, the final test asks whether:","options":["The product is cheap","The banker would invest his own money in the transaction","The regulator approves","It has a LIBOR benchmark"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 476.","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall Moore's questions","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[474,479]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.12","type":"application","prompt":"An Islamic bank's Shari’ah audit finds Rs.5m of revenue from non-compliant transactions. Per the author, who should bear the loss when it is sent to charity?","options":["Depositors","Shareholders","The central bank","Borrowers"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 474.","topicId":"t18.3.2.c","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply penalty principle","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[472,474]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.13","type":"mcq","prompt":"In the author’s diagnosis, which is a root cause of today’s socio-economic problems?","options":["Too little credit","Unbridled creation of fictitious assets, especially reserve currencies","Excessive gold reserves","Too many Islamic banks"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Along with unfettered self-interest and an interest-based system (p. 457).","topicId":"t18.1","chapter":18,"section":"18.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the author’s diagnosis","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.1","pages":[457,457]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.14","type":"identify","prompt":"Which 1986 publication described Islamic banking as first ridiculed in the West, then tolerated, then adopted?","options":["The Economist","South magazine","Financial Times","IMF Survey"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 458.","topicId":"t18.2","chapter":18,"section":"18.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the South survey","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2","pages":[457,459]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.15","type":"multi","prompt":"Which international measures does the author propose? (Select all.)","options":["More reserve currencies, including regional ones","Money and credit creation matched to real-sector growth","Replacing loan-based aid with investment and return-free disaster relief","A single global interest rate"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"Also aid that tangibly benefits recipients, and promoting Islamic finance as ethical banking (pp. 458–459).","topicId":"t18.2","chapter":18,"section":"18.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the international policy agenda","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2","pages":[457,459]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.16","type":"tf","prompt":"The author suggests the OIC as the best forum to develop a widely acceptable reserve currency.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"p. 458.","topicId":"t18.2","chapter":18,"section":"18.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall proposals on reserve currencies","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2","pages":[457,459]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.17","type":"scenario","prompt":"A Muslim country converts all its commercial banks to Islamic banking but keeps financing its budget with interest-bearing bonds. What does the author predict?","options":["Full transformation","Little visible impact on the economy","Collapse of the banks","Automatic real benchmarks"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 460.","topicId":"t18.2.1","chapter":18,"section":"18.2.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the author’s view on state-level adoption","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2.1","pages":[459,461]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.18","type":"order","prompt":"Order the requirements for phased transformation of an economy.","items":["Identify legal, regulatory and institutional requirements and policy components","Lay down a phased plan and implementation mechanism","Provide feedback and continuous review to remove obstacles"],"explanation":"p. 460.","topicId":"t18.2.1","chapter":18,"section":"18.2.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Sequence transformation steps","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2.1","pages":[459,461]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.19","type":"short","prompt":"Why does the author say it is almost impossible for IFIs to obtain Shari’ah-compliant benchmarks at present?","keywords":["government","interest","asset"],"correctAnswer":"Because governments and large entities still borrow on interest, so there is no large asset-based market (e.g. Ijarah, Istisna‘a, Shirkah financing by states) to generate real benchmarks.","explanation":"p. 460.","topicId":"t18.2.1","chapter":18,"section":"18.2.1","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain the benchmark problem","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2.1","pages":[459,461]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.20","type":"identify","prompt":"What distinguishes NCB Saudi Arabia’s “advance card”?","options":["Revolving interest credit","A prepaid line instead of a credit line","Based on ‘Inah","Only for corporates"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Attractive to women, the young and the self-employed (p. 461).","topicId":"t18.3","chapter":18,"section":"18.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall product innovations","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3","pages":[461,463]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.21","type":"match","prompt":"Match each institution to its role.","pairs":[["ARCIFI","Dispute resolution for IFIs (Dubai)"],["AAOIFI","Shari’ah, accounting and auditing standards"],["IFSB","Risk, capital adequacy and governance standards"],["LMC","Liquidity management"]],"explanation":"pp. 462–463.","topicId":"t18.3","chapter":18,"section":"18.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the support infrastructure","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3","pages":[461,463]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.22","type":"identify","prompt":"Which US legal change is linked to the growth of Islamic investment institutions in the USA?","options":["The Dodd–Frank Act","Repeal in 1999 of the Glass–Steagall Act of 1933","The Federal Reserve Act","Basel II"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 462.","topicId":"t18.3","chapter":18,"section":"18.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall regulatory drivers","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3","pages":[461,463]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.23","type":"multi","prompt":"Which fund categories does the author suggest? (Select all.)","options":["Low-variation funds on short-term Murabaha and leasing","Higher-return Musharakah or long-lease funds","Domestic and foreign currency trade funds","Guaranteed fixed-interest funds"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"p. 464.","topicId":"t18.3.1","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall fund management potential","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.1","pages":[463,465]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.24","type":"tf","prompt":"The author expects Islamic microfinance institutions to face fewer default, moral hazard and asymmetric-information problems.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"p. 465.","topicId":"t18.3.1","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Understand the potential of Islamic microfinance","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.1","pages":[463,465]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.25","type":"comparison","prompt":"For a high-net-worth client’s portfolio, how does the bank’s income differ as Mudarib and as agent?","options":["No difference","Mudarib: a pre-agreed share of profit; agent: a fee, with profit or loss going to the fund owners","Agent: share of profit; Mudarib: fee","Both earn interest"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 465.","topicId":"t18.3.1","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Compare Mudarib and agent roles","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.1","pages":[463,465]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.26","type":"identify","prompt":"Which areas does the author say have no Islamic alternative?","options":["Trade finance","Interest-based derivatives, short-selling and speculative FX transactions","Housing finance","Cards"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 466.","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall limits of product replication","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[465,469]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.27","type":"scenario","prompt":"A client defaults for two years. The bank wants compensation beyond the charity penalty. What approach does the chapter discuss?","options":["Charge the conventional opportunity cost","Claim loss through a court or arbitration, weighing the client’s finances, behaviour and litigation cost","Double the Murabaha price","Nothing can be done"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Some boards allow a rate based on income from a similar portfolio (p. 466).","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Evaluate options for liquidated damages","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[465,469]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.28","type":"tf","prompt":"Paying depositors fixed-looking returns by quietly shifting shareholders’ profit, without informing partners, is consistent with the Shari’ah as long as depositors benefit.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Profit equalisation reserves need disclosure. Apportioning profit just to compete without informing partners is against the spirit of the Shari’ah (p. 469).","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply integrity principles","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[465,469]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.29","type":"multi","prompt":"Which reasons for regulating IFIs does the chapter list? (Select all.)","options":["Making information available to investors","Protecting savers","Ensuring Shari’ah compliance and soundness","Guaranteeing fixed returns to depositors"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"Also a supportive legal framework and effective monetary policy (p. 470).","topicId":"t18.3.2.b","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall reasons for regulation","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[469,472]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.30","type":"identify","prompt":"According to the chapter, who should bear the risks of current accounts, especially when the bank is in distress?","options":["Investment account holders","The bank itself","The central bank","Depositors equally"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 471.","topicId":"t18.3.2.b","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall depositor protection","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[469,472]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.31","type":"scenario","prompt":"Murabaha purchases by an Islamic bank attract sales tax twice (on purchase and on sale to the client). What does the author recommend?","options":["Book it as a loan","Regulators should remove dual taxation and create a flexible tax framework","Skip the purchase","Charge the tax as interest"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 470.","topicId":"t18.3.2.b","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply tax recommendations","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[469,472]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.32","type":"identify","prompt":"Which framework option does the author consider best?","options":["A board in every IFI only","Accredited private Shari’ah firms only","A central Shari’ah board working with IFIs’ advisors, ideally with a forum of advisors","No Shari’ah oversight"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"p. 472.","topicId":"t18.3.2.c","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Evaluate compliance frameworks","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[472,474]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.33","type":"tf","prompt":"The author suggests that Shari’ah boards should only approve products and leave application to the bank.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"They should supervise and inspect at least once a year (p. 473).","topicId":"t18.3.2.c","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Understand the active role of Shari’ah boards","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[472,474]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.34","type":"application","prompt":"SBP’s Shari’ah audit finds revenue from non-compliant transactions. Who should bear the charity transfer?","options":["Depositors","Shareholders — depositors should not suffer for management’s failures","Split equally","The central bank"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 474.","topicId":"t18.3.2.c","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply penalty allocation rules","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[472,474]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.35","type":"short","prompt":"How does the chapter suggest resolving disputes between a bank’s Shari’ah department and Shari’ah auditors?","keywords":["manual","joint"],"correctAnswer":"By preparing a Shari’ah manual jointly with auditors/regulators, Shari’ah boards and practitioners, and auditing against it.","explanation":"p. 474.","topicId":"t18.3.2.c","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall audit dispute resolution","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[472,474]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.36","type":"identify","prompt":"According to the chapter, what is the greatest challenge for Islamic finance?","options":["Technology","Creating understanding and awareness of the Shari’ah principles among Muslims and non-Muslims","Tax","Branch networks"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 475.","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the main challenge","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[474,479]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.37","type":"multi","prompt":"What does prospering in a competitive environment require, according to the chapter? (Select all.)","options":["Shari’ah-conformant products","Best-practice screening and risk management","Innovative products and better service","Offering fixed guaranteed deposit returns"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"p. 477.","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall competitiveness requirements","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[474,479]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.38","type":"scenario","prompt":"Banking law requires deposits to be capital-guaranteed, while the Shari’ah requires PLS. What structural solution does the author suggest?","options":["Guarantee deposits anyway","Operate as mutual funds","Abolish deposits","Use Tawarruq"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 477.","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply structural solutions","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[474,479]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.39","type":"application","prompt":"Under the proposed Mudarabah lender-of-last-resort facility, what happens if the IFI fails to repay on the due date?","options":["Interest is charged","The profit-sharing ratio shifts in favour of the central bank, as provided in the agreement","The IFI is closed","Nothing"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 478.","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the Islamic LOLR proposal","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[474,479]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q18.40","type":"identify","prompt":"What does the author say Islamic banks may do with conventional banks offering Islamic business?","options":["Nothing","Invest funds with them if they invest on a compliant basis with segregation certified by a Shari’ah advisor","Lend at interest","Merge"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 478.","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall interbank rules","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[474,479]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q-ch18-1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What does the author identify as the root cause of major global socio-economic problems in this concluding chapter?","explanation":"The author restates his core thesis: socio-economic problems stem from unbridled creation of fictitious assets (particularly reserve currencies), unhindered demand/supply forces, exploitative individual sovereignty, unfettered self-interest, and the interest-based corrupt financial system.","topicId":"t18.1","chapter":18,"section":"18.1-18.2","topicLabel":"Macro-Level Policy Agenda","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the author's macro-level policy agenda for disciplining money/credit creation and financial systems","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.1","pages":[457]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Excessive Islamic banking regulation","The unbridled creation of fictitious assets, particularly reserve currencies, combined with unfettered self-interest and the interest-based financial system","A shortage of gold reserves worldwide","Overuse of Musharakah-based financing"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch18-2","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following are among the five international-level measures the author proposes?","explanation":"The author proposes five measures: diversifying reserve currencies (OIC-led), tying money creation to real sector growth, replacing loan-based finance, controlling corruption in foreign aid, and marketing Islamic finance as universal ethical banking - he does not propose abolishing central banks.","topicId":"t18.1","chapter":18,"section":"18.1-18.2","topicLabel":"Macro-Level Policy Agenda","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain the author's macro-level policy agenda for disciplining money/credit creation and financial systems","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2","pages":[458,459]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Increasing the number of reserve currencies, including a regional currency developed via the OIC","Tying money/credit creation to real sector development and growth potential","Replacing loan-based finance with direct investment, project financing and return-free calamity assistance","Abolishing all central banks worldwide immediately"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2]},{"id":"q-ch18-3","type":"short","prompt":"What example does the author use to illustrate the human cost of unbridled fictitious money creation, and what does he propose as a monetary policy model for tying money creation to real assets?","explanation":"This connects the chapter's macro-economic critique directly to a proposed Islamic-finance-based solution.","topicId":"t18.1","chapter":18,"section":"18.1-18.2","topicLabel":"Macro-Level Policy Agenda","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the author's macro-level policy agenda for disciplining money/credit creation and financial systems","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2","pages":[457,458,459]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"The author cites the Iraq War, financed largely through deficit financing/money creation, with a projected cost of about $1 trillion, as an example of harm enabled by fictitious money creation. He proposes using the principles of Sukuk issuance in Islamic finance as a model for transforming the basis of creating financial assets from 'thin air' to real underlying assets."},{"id":"q-ch18-4","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: The author states that most Muslim-majority countries have already adopted Islamic finance as an effective state-level policy objective.","explanation":"False - the author states only one or two countries may be considered exceptions; almost all Islamic countries still borrow through the conventional interest-based system, with only a negligible portion financed via Shari'ah-compliant instruments.","topicId":"t18.2.1","chapter":18,"section":"18.2.1","topicLabel":"Muslim States and Islamic Finance","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Describe the specific reforms the author recommends for Muslim states regarding Islamic finance adoption","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2.1","pages":[459,460]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch18-5","type":"mcq","prompt":"What three steps does the author recommend for a full economy-wide transformation toward Islamic finance?","explanation":"The author recommends: (i) identifying transformation requirements in laws, rules, regulations and institutional structures fitting an implementation timetable; (ii) laying down a phased plan and implementation mechanism; (iii) feedback/continuous review to ensure proper application and remove obstacles.","topicId":"t18.2.1","chapter":18,"section":"18.2.1","topicLabel":"Muslim States and Islamic Finance","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Describe the specific reforms the author recommends for Muslim states regarding Islamic finance adoption","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2.1","pages":[460]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Immediate abolition of all conventional banks, a public referendum, and a new constitution","Identifying legal/regulatory/institutional transformation requirements, a phased implementation plan, and continuous feedback/review","Only issuing more Sukuk with no other structural changes needed","Waiting for global consensus before taking any national action"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch18-6","type":"mcq","prompt":"What 1999 US regulatory change is credited with enabling the growth of over two dozen Islamic investment institutions in the USA?","explanation":"The chapter states that in the USA, more than two dozen Islamic investment institutions provide facilities to Riba-avoiding communities, a growth that began when the Glass-Steagall Act of 1933 was repealed in 1999.","topicId":"t18.3","chapter":18,"section":"18.3","topicLabel":"Growth Drivers and Global Footprint","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify the factors that have driven Islamic finance's rapid growth and its potential in fund management, Sukuk and specific sectors","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3","pages":[462]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Passage of the Dodd-Frank Act","Repeal of the Glass-Steagall Act of 1933","Creation of the Federal Reserve","The USA PATRIOT Act"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch18-7","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following are among the ten growth-driving factors identified in the chapter?","explanation":"The ten factors include religious demand, standardization (AAOIFI/IFSB), and Gulf petrodollar surplus growth, among others - there is no global ban on conventional banking mentioned.","topicId":"t18.3","chapter":18,"section":"18.3","topicLabel":"Growth Drivers and Global Footprint","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify the factors that have driven Islamic finance's rapid growth and its potential in fund management, Sukuk and specific sectors","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3","pages":[462,463]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Increasing demand for Riba-free instruments for religious reasons","Standardization via AAOIFI's Shari'ah Standards and IFSB's risk management/governance standards","Growth of surplus money in the Gulf region due to rising petroleum prices","A global ban on all conventional banking"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2]},{"id":"q-ch18-8","type":"comparison","prompt":"Compare the two broad categories of Islamic investment funds described in the chapter.","explanation":"The chapter distinguishes minimum-variation-return funds (short-term Murabaha and leasing operations, fixed earnings, suited to risk-averse savers) from high-return funds (Musharakah or long-term leasing, for those willing to take some risk).","topicId":"t18.3.1","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.1","topicLabel":"Promising Potential","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Identify the factors that have driven Islamic finance's rapid growth and its potential in fund management, Sukuk and specific sectors","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.1","pages":[463,464]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Minimum-variation-return funds (short-term Murabaha/leasing, suited to risk-averse savers) versus high-return funds (Musharakah/long-term leasing, for risk-tolerant investors)","Both fund types are identical in risk and return profile","Minimum-variation funds are always based on Musharakah","High-return funds cannot legally be offered by Islamic banks"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch18-9","type":"short","prompt":"What advantage does the chapter claim Islamic microfinance institutions (IMFIs) would have over conventional microfinance, and what additional funding sources can they draw on?","explanation":"This is drawn from the chapter's discussion of potential in specific sectors, citing Habib Ahmed's IDB study.","topicId":"t18.3.1","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.1","topicLabel":"Promising Potential","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Identify the factors that have driven Islamic finance's rapid growth and its potential in fund management, Sukuk and specific sectors","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.1","pages":[465]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"IMFIs would have fewer default, moral hazard and asymmetric information problems. They can also draw on income from Zakat and Waqf institutions to directly finance needy small business enterprises without involving a middleman."},{"id":"q-ch18-10","type":"mcq","prompt":"How does the author characterize the significance of differing Shari'ah interpretation as an obstacle to Islamic finance's development?","explanation":"The author argues Shari'ah interpretation 'is not as big an issue as some people consider; it is no longer a real problem,' since the OIC Islamic Fiqh Council, AAOIFI and Shari'ah scholars generally have already discussed almost all major areas at length.","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","topicLabel":"Shari'ah Interpretation and Integrity/Credibility","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Analyse the key structural, regulatory and credibility issues facing Islamic finance's continued development","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[465,466]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["As the single greatest unsolved obstacle facing the industry","As 'not as big an issue as some people consider,' since major areas have already been resolved by the OIC Islamic Fiqh Council and AAOIFI","As an obstacle that can never be resolved by any institution","As irrelevant and never worth discussing"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch18-11","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank wants to claim liquidated damages from a defaulting client through court action. According to the chapter, what should NOT be used as the basis for such a court award?","explanation":"The chapter explicitly states 'conventional opportunity cost per se should not be the basis for such award,' while noting some Shari'ah boards allow charging a rate based on the bank's income from a similar portfolio, with courts weighing the client's financial position/behaviour and litigation costs/time.","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","topicLabel":"Shari'ah Interpretation and Integrity/Credibility","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Analyse the key structural, regulatory and credibility issues facing Islamic finance's continued development","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[466]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["The bank's income from a similar portfolio in the relevant period","Conventional opportunity cost per se","The financial position and behaviour of the client","Expenses and time involved in litigation"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch18-12","type":"short","prompt":"What four factors determine an Islamic financial institution's integrity, per the chapter?","explanation":"These four factors structure the chapter's discussion of integrity and credibility.","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","topicLabel":"Shari'ah Interpretation and Integrity/Credibility","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Analyse the key structural, regulatory and credibility issues facing Islamic finance's continued development","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[467]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"(1) The status of Shari'ah compliance of their products; (2) the impact of the products on clients and society/economy; (3) professional competence and care for stakeholder interests; (4) behaviour toward and observance of Shari'ah norms by IFIs' incumbents."},{"id":"q-ch18-13","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why does the chapter say Islamic banks face an uneven playing field regarding taxation compared to conventional banks?","explanation":"Since Islamic banks must purchase assets for onward sale/lease, taxation and fees on those purchases create an uneven playing field, sometimes leading banks to adopt Shari'ah-compliance-questionable practices to avoid such costs.","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","topicLabel":"Structure and Regulatory/Tax Issues","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Analyse the key structural, regulatory and credibility issues facing Islamic finance's continued development","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[470]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Islamic banks pay no taxes at all in most countries","Islamic banks must purchase assets for onward sale or lease, so taxation/fees on those purchases create additional cost burdens conventional lending doesn't incur","Conventional banks are exempt from all forms of regulation","There is no difference in tax treatment anywhere in the world"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch18-14","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: The AAOIFI's Murabaha Standard is named 'Murabaha to Purchase Orderer' specifically because Islamic banks are required to hold full inventory of every good they finance.","explanation":"False - the standard's name reflects the opposite: banks purchase goods upon client requisition for onward sale, and are not required to hold full inventory, which is neither a Shari'ah requirement nor unusual real-sector practice.","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","topicLabel":"Structure and Regulatory/Tax Issues","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Analyse the key structural, regulatory and credibility issues facing Islamic finance's continued development","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[469]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch18-15","type":"comparison","prompt":"Compare the three Shari'ah compliance framework options the chapter discusses, and identify the author's preferred option.","explanation":"The chapter weighs a full board per IFI (costly, scholar shortage), independent private-sector boards (weaker operational monitoring/guidance), and a central jurisdiction-level board coordinating with individual bank advisors - the author's preferred option, especially combined with a forum of Shari'ah advisors.","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","topicLabel":"Shari'ah Compliance Framework","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Evaluate the options for a Shari'ah compliance framework and the major challenges facing the industry's future","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[471,472]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Option 1: full board per IFI (costly, scholar shortage). Option 2: independent private-sector boards (weaker operational monitoring). Option 3: central jurisdiction-level board + individual advisors (author's preference)","The chapter presents only one option with no alternatives","The author prefers having no Shari'ah oversight structure at all","All three options are presented as equally preferable with no distinction made"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch18-16","type":"short","prompt":"Describe the key elements of the State Bank of Pakistan's Shari'ah compliance framework, as detailed in Box 18.1.","explanation":"This reflects the specific framework detailed in Box 18.1, illustrating the author's preferred central-board model in practice.","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","topicLabel":"Shari'ah Compliance Framework","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Evaluate the options for a Shari'ah compliance framework and the major challenges facing the industry's future","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[473,474]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"A central Shari'ah board (two Shari'ah scholars, three experts in banking, accounting and legal framework) was established at SBP in December 2003 to advise on modes, procedures, laws and regulation. Each Islamic Banking Institution must appoint at least a Shari'ah advisor per SBP-approved fit-and-proper criteria and conduct internal Shari'ah audit at least annually. SBP also conducts its own periodic Shari'ah compliance inspection via trained inspection staff, using a manual developed in consultation with a reputable consultancy firm."},{"id":"q-ch18-17","type":"scenario","prompt":"An Islamic bank's assets-side transaction is found to be non-Shari'ah-compliant, and the revenue must go to the Charity Account. Who should bear this resulting loss - shareholders or depositors, and why?","explanation":"The chapter states this loss should belong to shareholders, not depositors, because depositors furnish deposits for Shari'ah-compliant business and if the bank fails to accomplish this, it must be penalized - the depositors should not be penalized for follies of the bank's management.","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","topicLabel":"Shari'ah Compliance Framework","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Evaluate the options for a Shari'ah compliance framework and the major challenges facing the industry's future","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[474]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Depositors, since they receive the returns from the bank's operations","Shareholders, since depositors furnish funds expecting Shari'ah-compliant business and should not be penalized for the bank management's failures","Neither party bears any loss in this situation","The central bank always absorbs such losses"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch18-18","type":"mcq","prompt":"What does M. Nejatullah Siddiqi argue should be added to a comprehensive redefinition of 'Shari'ah-compliant'?","explanation":"Siddiqi argues the term should not be confined to analogical reasoning and matching new with old approved contracts; Maslaha and Maqasid al-Shari'ah should be essential parts of a comprehensive definition, requiring strong involvement of trained economists and social scientists.","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","topicLabel":"Education, Compliance and Competitiveness Challenges","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Evaluate the options for a Shari'ah compliance framework and the major challenges facing the industry's future","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[475]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Nothing - the current definition is already comprehensive enough","Considerations of Maslaha (public interest) and Maqasid al-Shari'ah (objectives of Shari'ah), requiring economists/social scientists alongside traditionally-trained scholars","Only conventional financial engineering techniques","A requirement that all products must replicate conventional instruments exactly"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch18-19","type":"short","prompt":"What four questions does Phillip Moore say a Shari'ah board will typically ask about a proposed transaction?","explanation":"If the answer to any of these is no, the transaction would usually be rejected, though the committee's formal power to reject rests only on Shari'ah non-compliance grounds.","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","topicLabel":"Education, Compliance and Competitiveness Challenges","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Evaluate the options for a Shari'ah compliance framework and the major challenges facing the industry's future","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[476]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"(1) Do the terms of the transaction comply with Shari'ah law? (2) Is this the best investment for the client? (3) Does the investment produce value for the client and for the community/society? (4) As an asset manager, is this a transaction the banker would be prepared to invest their own money in?"},{"id":"q-ch18-20","type":"scenario","prompt":"A jurisdiction's banking law requires deposits to be capital-guaranteed, which conflicts with Shari'ah's requirement of profit/loss sharing. What structural solution does the chapter propose?","explanation":"The chapter proposes that this legal-mismatch problem could be resolved if IFIs operate in the form of mutual funds, enabling them to earn higher profits as real-sector businesses normally earn, and pass on a greater part of the profits so earned to savers/investors.","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","topicLabel":"Education, Compliance and Competitiveness Challenges","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Evaluate the options for a Shari'ah compliance framework and the major challenges facing the industry's future","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[477]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Islamic banks should simply violate the banking law","IFIs could operate in mutual-fund form, enabling higher real-sector-typical profits and passing a greater profit share to savers/investors","The conflict is unsolvable and IFIs cannot operate in such jurisdictions","Islamic banks should abandon profit/loss sharing entirely"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch18-21","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why does the chapter say IFIs face a competitive disadvantage regarding statutory liquidity reserves (SLR)?","explanation":"Compared with conventional institutions, IFIs have to place a greater part of their deposits with the central bank on which they get no return in lieu of the statutory liquidity reserves (SLR), harming their competitiveness.","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","topicLabel":"Benchmarks, Product Development, Liquidity and Human Resources","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Evaluate the options for a Shari'ah compliance framework and the major challenges facing the industry's future","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[478]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["IFIs are exempt from SLR requirements entirely","IFIs must place a greater portion of deposits as SLR than conventional banks and earn no return on them","SLR requirements apply only to conventional banks","IFIs receive a premium return on their SLR placements"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch18-22","type":"short","prompt":"Describe the two options the author proposes for an Islamic-framework central bank lender-of-last-resort mechanism.","explanation":"These are the two specific mechanisms the chapter proposes to replace the conventional lender-of-last-resort function within Shari'ah principles.","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","topicLabel":"Benchmarks, Product Development, Liquidity and Human Resources","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Evaluate the options for a Shari'ah compliance framework and the major challenges facing the industry's future","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[478,479]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"(1) A Mudarabah-based facility for a defined-extent liquidity crisis (e.g. two or three days) with a profit-sharing ratio (e.g. 50:50) on a daily-product basis, which could shift more favourably toward the central bank if the IFI cannot repay by the due date. (2) A common pool established at the central bank, funded by all IFIs contributing a specified percentage of their deposits, giving members the right to interest-free accommodation for a defined short period (e.g. up to three days) during a liquidity crunch."},{"id":"q-ch18-23","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: The author's conclusion states that fixity of rates or rentals is itself a Shari'ah problem that Islamic finance must eliminate.","explanation":"False - the author reiterates that fixity of rates is 'no problem at all'; nonfixity of prices and rentals would actually make the transactions invalid per Shari'ah rules.","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"section":"18.4","topicLabel":"Conclusion","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Synthesize the book's overall conclusions and recommendations for Islamic banking and finance's sustainable growth","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.4","pages":[479]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch18-24","type":"short","prompt":"How does Zubair Hasan's quoted statement frame Islamic banking, and what is the book's final specific recommendation?","explanation":"This closing framing and recommendation ties the chapter's conclusion back to its opening macro-level policy agenda.","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"section":"18.4","topicLabel":"Conclusion","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Synthesize the book's overall conclusions and recommendations for Islamic banking and finance's sustainable growth","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.4","pages":[479,480]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Zubair Hasan frames Islamic banking as 'an on-going process in a social milieu characterized with mass poverty and gross inequalities in the wealth, income and opportunities' - a continuing developmental process, not a finished achievement. The book's final specific recommendation is that the use of Islamic finance principles for public sector financing needs more attention, since it could help ensure fiscal discipline and provide a just basis for monetary management."},{"id":"q-ch18-25","type":"mcq","prompt":"What does the author say IFIs must do to achieve genuine socio-economic impact, beyond simply avoiding the word 'interest'?","explanation":"The author states IFIs should look beyond the formal abolition of interest and practically involve themselves in real business activities to achieve positive socio-economic implications through real sector development and just, equitable pricing policies.","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"section":"18.4","topicLabel":"Conclusion","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Synthesize the book's overall conclusions and recommendations for Islamic banking and finance's sustainable growth","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.4","pages":[479,480]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Nothing further is required beyond formally avoiding interest","Look beyond the formal abolition of interest and practically involve themselves in real business activities","Merge entirely with conventional banks","Focus exclusively on maximizing shareholder profit"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch18-26","type":"mcq","prompt":"Per the chapter's discussion of Islamic banks' 'structure,' why does the AAOIFI's Murabaha Standard carry the title 'Murabaha to Purchase Orderer'?","explanation":"Islamic banks' modus operandi differs from the general trading business: they normally do not hold inventories, but purchase goods/assets on client requisition for onward letting or sale - reflected directly in the Murabaha Standard's title.","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","topicLabel":"Structure of Financial Institutions","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Analyse the key structural, regulatory and credibility issues facing Islamic finance's continued development","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[469]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Because Islamic banks are legally required to maintain large inventories of goods","Because it reflects that Islamic banks purchase goods on client requisition for onward sale/lease rather than holding inventory like ordinary traders","Because it is simply an arbitrary naming convention with no substantive meaning","Because it refers exclusively to export financing"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch18-27","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following are among the reasons the chapter gives for why Islamic financial institutions need regulation?","explanation":"The chapter lists five reasons for regulation: investor information, protecting savers, Shari'ah compliance/soundness, a workable legal framework given the 'no cost of funds recovery' rule, and effective monetary policy/management - not guaranteeing higher IFI profits.","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","topicLabel":"Regulatory and Tax Issues","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Analyse the key structural, regulatory and credibility issues facing Islamic finance's continued development","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[469,470]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Making needed information available to investors","Ensuring Shari'ah compliance and soundness of the financial system","Making the legal framework workable in a system where 'cost of funds' cannot be recovered on default","Guaranteeing IFIs a higher profit margin than conventional banks"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2]},{"id":"q-ch18-28","type":"short","prompt":"Describe the two practice-related (not purely Shari'ah-interpretation) unresolved issues the chapter identifies.","explanation":"These are the two 'unresolved issues' the chapter attributes to practical difficulties and ground realities rather than pure Shari'ah interpretation.","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","topicLabel":"Shari'ah Interpretation and Integrity/Credibility","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Analyse the key structural, regulatory and credibility issues facing Islamic finance's continued development","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[466]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"(1) On what basis courts should award liquidated damages to a bank for a defaulting client's receivables - conventional opportunity cost per se should not be the basis, though some Shari'ah boards allow charging a rate based on the bank's income from a similar portfolio, with courts weighing the client's financial position/behaviour and litigation costs/time. (2) The liability of a bank's shareholders towards investment account holders when losses occur due to overexposure, lack of diversification, imprudent banking practices, or actual business losses - requiring a firm regulatory framework safeguarding depositors' interests."},{"id":"q-ch18-29","type":"mcq","prompt":"How does the chapter suggest Islamic banks cover short-term mismatches between client demand for funds and their supply?","explanation":"Any mismatch between clients' demand for funds and their supply is normally covered through inter-bank transactions, conducted on the Mudarabah principle; establishing such a market in each jurisdiction is a key element for short-term liquidity management.","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","topicLabel":"Benchmarks, Product Development, Liquidity and Human Resources","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Evaluate the options for a Shari'ah compliance framework and the major challenges facing the industry's future","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[478]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Through conventional interbank lending at LIBOR","Through an inter-bank Islamic money market conducted on the Mudarabah principle","Islamic banks cannot address such mismatches at all","By suspending withdrawals until funds are available"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch18-30","type":"mcq","prompt":"What does the chapter identify as one of the major bottlenecks for the Islamic finance industry's development at its current stage?","explanation":"The chapter identifies scarcity of trained human resources as a major bottleneck; incumbents of IFIs need a significantly different mindset, special orientation, technical competence, and awareness of Islamic finance principles.","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","topicLabel":"Benchmarks, Product Development, Liquidity and Human Resources","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Evaluate the options for a Shari'ah compliance framework and the major challenges facing the industry's future","source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[479]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Excess of trained human resources","Scarcity of trained human resources with the right mindset and orientation, technically competent and well aware of Islamic finance principles","Too many universities offering Islamic finance courses","Lack of demand from Muslim investors"],"correctAnswer":1}],"flashcards":[{"id":"f18.1","front":"Author's diagnosis (Ch 18)","back":"Unbridled creation of fictitious assets (reserve currencies), unfettered self-interest and interest-based finance cause socio-economic problems.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t18.1","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.1","pages":[457,457]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f18.2","front":"Five international policy measures","back":"More reserve currencies (OIC); money creation matched to real assets; replace loan-based finance; honest aid; ethical/religious appeal.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t18.2","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2","pages":[457,459]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f18.3","front":"Phased transformation of economies","back":"(i) Identify legal/regulatory/institutional requirements; (ii) phased plan and implementation mechanism; (iii) continuous review.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t18.2.1","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2.1","pages":[459,461]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f18.4","front":"Driving forces of Islamic finance (any five)","back":"Riba-free demand; cultural resurgence; scholars' involvement; products for liquidity; AAOIFI/IFSB standards; Gulf surpluses; regulatory flexibility; conversions; HNWIs; ethical nature.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t18.3","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3","pages":[461,463]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f18.5","front":"Fund management potential","back":"Low-risk Murabaha/leasing funds; high-return Musharakah funds; restricted accounts; equity funds; domestic and international trade funds.","category":"Capital markets","topicId":"t18.3.1","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.1","pages":[463,465]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f18.6","front":"Integrity of IFIs depends on","back":"Shari’ah compliance of products; impact on clients/society; professional competence; ethical conduct of staff.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[465,469]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f18.7","front":"Reasons IFIs need regulation","back":"Information for investors; saver protection; Shari’ah compliance and soundness; legal framework without cost-of-funds recovery; effective monetary policy.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t18.3.2.b","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[469,472]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f18.8","front":"Shari’ah compliance framework options","back":"Board/advisor per IFI (+ national forum); accredited private boards; central Shari’ah board (preferred, with advisors' forum).","category":"Banking","topicId":"t18.3.2.c","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[472,474]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f18.9","front":"SBP framework (Box 18.1)","back":"SBP Shari’ah board Dec 2003 (2 scholars + 3 experts); IBI board/advisor fit & proper; annual internal Shari’ah audit; SBP inspection manual.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t18.3.2.c","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[472,474]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f18.10","front":"Moore's four questions for a Shari’ah board","back":"Shari’ah-compliant? Best investment for client? Creates value for client and society? Would the banker invest his own money?","category":"Principles","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[474,479]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f18.11","front":"Islamic lender-of-last-resort options","back":"Short Mudarabah facility (e.g. 50:50 daily product, ratio rises if late) or a common IFI pool at the central bank giving interest-free accommodation up to 3 days.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[474,479]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f18.12","front":"Siddiqi on redefining ‘Shari’ah-compliant’","back":"Not only analogical matching of contracts — include Maslaha and Maqasid al-Shari’ah, with economists and social scientists involved.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[474,479]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f18.13","front":"Three remedies in the author’s diagnosis","back":"Discipline money creation; temper self-interest with social interest and ethics; free finance from exploitation and chance (p. 457).","category":"Principles","topicId":"t18.1","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.1","pages":[457,457]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f18.14","front":"South magazine (1986) on Islamic banking","back":"First ridiculed in the West, then tolerated, then adopted after scrutiny (p. 458).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t18.2","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2","pages":[457,459]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f18.15","front":"Real benchmarks — how?","back":"If states finance through asset-based Ijarah, Istisna‘a and Shirkah (e.g. via OIC/IDB packages), each economy gets real benchmarks for pricing (p. 460).","category":"Principles","topicId":"t18.2.1","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2.1","pages":[459,461]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f18.16","front":"Islamic finance support infrastructure","back":"IDB, IFSB, IIFM, GCIBAFI, ARCIFI (Dubai, disputes), AAOIFI, IIRA, LMC; Bahrain is the Middle East hub (p. 462).","category":"Banking","topicId":"t18.3","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3","pages":[461,463]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f18.17","front":"Islamic microfinance advantages","back":"Fewer default, moral hazard and asymmetric-information problems; can use Zakat and Waqf; Habib Ahmed’s IDB study (p. 465).","category":"Banking","topicId":"t18.3.1","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.1","pages":[463,465]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f18.18","front":"Innovation ≠ replication","back":"Islamic finance should not copy every conventional product; interest derivatives, short-selling and speculative FX have no alternatives (p. 466).","category":"Principles","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[465,469]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f18.19","front":"Profit smoothing and integrity","back":"Profit equalisation reserves are fine with disclosure; secretly shifting shareholders’ profit to match rivals is against the spirit of the Shari’ah (p. 469).","category":"Banking","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[465,469]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f18.20","front":"Regulatory steps listed (selected)","back":"Avoid dual tax; a Shari’ah framework; fair profit allocation; more information; prudential rules; current-account risk on the bank; supervisor training; research centre; rating and feasibility bodies (pp. 470–471).","category":"Banking","topicId":"t18.3.2.b","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[469,472]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f18.21","front":"Shari’ah inspection options","back":"The central bank itself, private Shari’ah audit firms or external CA firms; choice depends on local expertise (Chapra and Khan) (p. 473).","category":"Banking","topicId":"t18.3.2.c","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[472,474]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f18.22","front":"Who bears non-compliant revenue?","back":"Shareholders, not depositors, since the bank failed its mandate (p. 474).","category":"Banking","topicId":"t18.3.2.c","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[472,474]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f18.23","front":"SLR disadvantage of IFIs","back":"They hold a greater share of deposits with the central bank without return, which hurts competitiveness (p. 478).","category":"Banking","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[474,479]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f18.24","front":"Zubair Hasan on Islamic banking","back":"An on-going process in a social milieu of mass poverty and gross inequality (p. 480).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[474,479]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-1","front":"What does the author identify as the root cause of major socio-economic problems in the book's concluding chapter?","back":"The unbridled creation of fictitious assets, particularly reserve currencies, combined with unhindered demand/supply forces, exploitative individual sovereignty, unfettered self-interest, and the interest-based financial system.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t18.1","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.1","pages":[457]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-2","front":"Which international forum does the author suggest is best placed to develop a widely-acceptable reserve currency for the Muslim world?","back":"The OIC (Organization of the Islamic Conference).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t18.2","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2","pages":[458]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-3","front":"What four alternatives does the author propose to replace loan-based finance for developing countries?","back":"(i) direct investment in commodity/service sectors; (ii) project-based financing under transparent standards; (iii) sovereign/multinational portfolio investment strengthening recipient equity markets; (iv) return-free or in-kind assistance for natural calamities.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t18.2","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2","pages":[459]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-4","front":"How many Muslim-majority countries does the author say are playing an effective policy-level role in promoting Islamic finance?","back":"Only one or two countries may be considered exceptions; almost all Islamic countries still borrow through the conventional interest-based system.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t18.2.1","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2.1","pages":[459,460]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-5","front":"Which institutions does the author suggest could formulate financial packages obliging member countries to use asset-based financing?","back":"The OIC or IDB (Islamic Development Bank), obliging member countries to resort to asset-based financing on the basis of Ijarah, Istisna'a and Shirkah.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t18.2.1","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2.1","pages":[460]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-6","front":"What US legislative change in 1999 is credited with enabling growth of over two dozen Islamic investment institutions in the USA?","back":"The repeal of the Glass-Steagall Act of 1933.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t18.3","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3","pages":[462]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-7","front":"Name three major Western financial institutions cited as increasingly offering Shari'ah-compliant products.","back":"Any three of: Citigroup, Deutsche Bank, ABN AMRO, HSBC, UBS.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t18.3","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3","pages":[461]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-8","front":"What two broad categories of Islamic investment funds does the chapter describe?","back":"Minimum-variation-return funds (based on short-term Murabaha and leasing, for risk-averse savers) and high-return funds (based on Musharakah or long-term leasing, for risk-tolerant investors).","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t18.3.1","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.1","pages":[463,464]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-9","front":"Which two institutions' experience is cited as showing SME/trade-focused Islamic funds can achieve the lowest default ratios?","back":"Grameen Bank of Bangladesh and the Bangladesh Islamic Bank.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t18.3.1","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.1","pages":[464]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-10","front":"Does the author consider Shari'ah interpretation differences a major obstacle to Islamic finance's development?","back":"No - he argues it is 'not as big an issue as some people consider,' since the OIC Islamic Fiqh Council, AAOIFI and Shari'ah scholars have already discussed and resolved almost all major areas.","category":"Capital markets","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[465,466]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-11","front":"What are the four factors the chapter identifies as determining an Islamic financial institution's integrity?","back":"(1) Shari'ah compliance status of its products; (2) impact of products on clients/society/economy; (3) professional competence and stakeholder care; (4) incumbents' behaviour toward/observance of Shari'ah norms.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[467]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-12","front":"What is the AAOIFI's Murabaha Standard officially named, and what does it reflect about Islamic banks' structural identity?","back":"'Murabaha to Purchase Orderer' - reflecting that Islamic banks purchase goods upon client requisition for onward sale rather than holding full inventory, distinguishing their procedure from conventional lending while remaining financial intermediaries.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[469]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-13","front":"List three of the ten specific regulatory/tax reforms the chapter recommends for Islamic finance.","back":"Any three of: saving IFIs from dual taxation, facilitating Shari'ah requirements, providing an effective compliance framework, ensuring justifiable profit distribution, increasing investor information, enforcing suited prudential rules, isolating current-account risk from investment accounts, training central bank staff, establishing a research/training centre, providing rating/feasibility infrastructure.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[470,471]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-14","front":"Which Shari'ah compliance framework option does the author prefer?","back":"A central Shari'ah board at the country/jurisdiction level, coordinating with individual banks' Shari'ah advisors/boards - ideally combined with a forum where advisors periodically meet to discuss Shari'ah issues.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[472]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-15","front":"When was the State Bank of Pakistan's central Shari'ah board established, and what is its composition?","back":"December 2003; comprising two Shari'ah scholars and three experts in banking, accounting and the legal framework.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[473]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-16","front":"If an IFI's revenue from a non-Shari'ah-compliant transaction is lost to the Charity Account, who should ultimately bear that loss - shareholders or depositors?","back":"Shareholders - since depositors furnish funds expecting Shari'ah-compliant business and should not be penalized for the bank management's failures.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[474]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-17","front":"What does M. Nejatullah Siddiqi argue should be added to the definition of 'Shari'ah-compliant'?","back":"Considerations of Maslaha (public interest) and Maqasid al-Shari'ah (objectives of Shari'ah), not just analogical matching of new contracts to old approved ones - requiring strong involvement of trained economists and social scientists.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[475]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-18","front":"What are Phillip Moore's four questions a Shari'ah board typically asks about a transaction?","back":"(1) Does it comply with Shari'ah law? (2) Is it the best investment for the client? (3) Does it produce value for the client and community/society? (4) Would the banker personally invest their own money in it?","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[476]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-19","front":"Why do Islamic banks face a competitive disadvantage regarding statutory liquidity reserves (SLR)?","back":"IFIs must place a greater portion of deposits with central banks as SLR compared to conventional banks, and earn NO RETURN on them - harming their ability to offer competitive returns to depositors.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[478]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-20","front":"What two options does the author propose for an Islamic-framework central bank lender-of-last-resort mechanism?","back":"(1) A Mudarabah-based facility for a defined short-term liquidity crisis (e.g. 2-3 days, profit-sharing ratio like 50:50); (2) a common pool at the central bank funded by IFI contributions, giving members interest-free accommodation rights for a defined short period (e.g. up to 3 days).","category":"Banking","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[478,479]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-21","front":"What percentage threshold applies to cash/receivables in a securitized pool for it to maintain a genuine secondary market, per this chapter?","back":"The cash and receivables component should be less than 50% of the total assets of the pool or fund.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[478]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-22","front":"Does the author consider fixity of rates/prices in Islamic finance a Shari'ah problem?","back":"No - he reiterates in the conclusion that fixity of rates is not a problem at all; nonfixity of prices/rentals would actually make many transactions invalid under Shari'ah rules.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.4","pages":[479]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-23","front":"How does Zubair Hasan frame Islamic banking in the quote the author closes with?","back":"As 'an on-going process in a social milieu characterized with mass poverty and gross inequalities in the wealth, income and opportunities' - a continuing developmental process, not a finished achievement.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.4","pages":[480]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-24","front":"What are the five international-level policy measures the author proposes to reform the global financial system?","back":"(1) Diversifying reserve currencies, with the OIC developing a regional Muslim-world reserve currency; (2) tying money/credit creation to real sector development (Sukuk principles as a model); (3) replacing loan-based finance with direct investment, project financing, portfolio investment and return-free calamity assistance; (4) controlling corruption in foreign aid so it tangibly benefits recipients; (5) marketing Islamic finance as universal religious/ethical banking to mobilize untapped savings.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t18.2","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2","pages":[458,459]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-25","front":"Do Islamic banks hold inventories of goods like ordinary trading businesses, per the chapter's discussion of bank 'structure'?","back":"No - although Islamic banks must deal in goods (unlike conventional banks, which deal in money), they normally don't hold inventory; they purchase goods/assets on client requisition for onward letting or sale. There is no Shari'ah objection to this, and it is reflected in AAOIFI's Murabaha Standard being titled 'Murabaha to Purchase Orderer'.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[469]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-26","front":"What five reasons does the chapter give for why Islamic financial institutions need regulation, just like conventional ones?","back":"(1) Making needed information available to investors; (2) protecting savers' interests; (3) ensuring Shari'ah compliance and financial system soundness; (4) making the legal framework workable where 'cost of funds' cannot be recovered on default; (5) making monetary policy and management effective.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t18.3.2","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[469,470]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-27","front":"How can Islamic banks cover short-term mismatches between client demand for funds and their supply, per the chapter's liquidity management discussion?","back":"Through an inter-bank Islamic money market conducted on the Mudarabah principle. Establishing such a market in each jurisdiction with a significant number of IFIs is identified as a key element for short-term liquidity and monetary management.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[478]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-28","front":"What early effort does the chapter cite toward developing Shari'ah-compliant benchmarks for pricing and profit-sharing ratios?","back":"A 1998 study by Abbas Mirakhor and Nadeem ul Haque, focused on developing indices for calculating rates of return on National Participation Papers (NPP).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[477]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch18-29","front":"What alternative to a conventional credit card has National Commercial Bank of Saudi Arabia introduced, and who is it especially attractive to?","back":"An 'advance card' - a prepaid line rather than a credit line, with added benefits like purchase protection and travel accident insurance without interest or extra fees. It is especially attractive to women, the young, the self-employed, and small-establishment employees who may not meet strict conventional credit card requirements.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t18.3","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3","pages":[461]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}],"exam":[{"id":"e18.1","kind":"long","prompt":"Discuss the potential, issues and challenges facing Islamic banking, and propose a way forward.","expectedStructure":["Driving forces","Potential: funds, Sukuk, SMEs/microfinance","Issues: interpretation, integrity, structure, regulation, compliance frameworks","Challenges (seven)","Recommendations"],"keyConcepts":["integrity","benchmarks","awareness"],"essentialPoints":["Awareness is the greatest challenge"],"commonMistakes":["Listing without evaluation"],"topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[474,479]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e18.2","kind":"short","prompt":"Compare options for a national Shari’ah compliance framework and evaluate SBP's model.","expectedStructure":["Board/advisor per IFI","Private accredited boards","Central board + forum","Inspection options","Box 18.1","Penalties for lapses"],"keyConcepts":["fit and proper","Shari’ah audit"],"essentialPoints":["Boards must supervise, not only approve"],"commonMistakes":["Ignoring cost and scarcity of scholars"],"topicId":"t18.3.2.c","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[472,474]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e18.3","kind":"conceptual","prompt":"What role should Muslim states and international institutions play in promoting Islamic finance?","expectedStructure":["Diagnosis","Reserve currencies and money creation","Replacing loan-based finance","OIC/IDB packages","Phased transformation"],"keyConcepts":["fictitious assets","public finance"],"essentialPoints":["Real benchmarks from sovereign Islamic instruments"],"commonMistakes":["Focusing only on retail banking"],"topicId":"t18.2.1","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.2.1","pages":[459,461]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e18.4","kind":"viva","prompt":"Why do Islamic banks still use interest-based benchmarks, and how can this change?","expectedStructure":["Small market share","Lack of sovereign Islamic instruments","Research on benchmarks","Public finance reform"],"keyConcepts":["benchmark"],"essentialPoints":["Permissible as a pricing tool"],"commonMistakes":["Saying benchmarks make contracts interest-based"],"topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[474,479]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e18.5","kind":"scenario","prompt":"You advise a central bank introducing Islamic banking. Propose its Shari’ah compliance framework and regulatory priorities.","expectedStructure":["Framework options","Recommended model","Inspection","Regulatory steps","Liquidity tools"],"keyConcepts":["Central Shari’ah board","Shari’ah audit","Dual taxation","LOLR"],"essentialPoints":["Compare the four options (pp. 472–473)","Central board plus advisors plus forum (p. 472)","SBP Box 18.1 as a model (pp. 473–474)","Shareholders bear non-compliance (p. 474)","Regulatory steps (pp. 470–471)","Mudarabah LOLR or common pool (p. 478)"],"commonMistakes":["A passive Shari’ah board","Charging non-compliance to depositors"],"topicId":"t18.3.2.c","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.2","pages":[472,474]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"et-18","kind":"answer-plan","prompt":"Summarize the main challenges facing Islamic banking and finance identified in the book's concluding chapter.","expectedStructure":["Note the book frames both promising potential and real challenges","List several challenge themes","Conclude with why addressing them matters for the industry's credibility"],"keyConcepts":["Potential of Islamic banking","Issues in Islamic finance","Challenges"],"essentialPoints":["The book balances a promising-potential assessment of Islamic banking with a candid discussion of issues and challenges still facing the industry.","These include gaps between theory and practice, the industry's continued reliance on debt-creating modes rather than genuine risk-sharing, and the need to develop dedicated (non-interest) benchmarks.","The concluding agenda calls for coordinated action from policymakers, regulators, and Shari'ah scholars to close these gaps and realize Islamic finance's socio-economic objectives."],"commonMistakes":["Presenting the book's tone as purely promotional — Chapter 18 explicitly discusses real, unresolved issues and challenges, not just potential.","Omitting the policy-agenda dimension (the role of states/regulators), which the book treats as central to the way forward."],"topicId":"t18.3.3","chapter":18,"source":{"chapter":18,"section":"18.3.3","pages":[474]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}]});
